window.COURSE_SEARCH=[{"title": "Module 00: Orientation: Business Partner Capstone and AI Value", "url": "module-00.html", "summary": "Trace a semester-adaptive capstone from a partner or classroom business need through design, implementation, controlled deployment, testing, and a sales or adoption pitch.", "text": "Trace a semester-adaptive capstone from a partner or classroom business need through design, implementation, controlled deployment, testing, and a sales or adoption pitch. Connect a business measure to the problem, user, and decision it supports. Scope a Finance, Marketing, MBA/strategy, MIS, or Operations solution to available evidence, data, tools, time, and partner access. The course is organized around a business capstone. Each semester, Memari selects or supplies a problem brief based on the partner’s need and the available data, tools, budget, skills, time, and access. Students discover the problem, design an AI-assisted solution, implement a minimum usable product or service with existing no-code tools, deploy it in a controlled setting, test it with a user, and pitch a supported sales or adoption decision. The core learning outcomes and rubrics stay stable while the project changes. Statistics, tables, and figures help judge the solution; they are not the final product by themselves. Published cases are practice options; follow Memari’s confirmed semester brief for the actual partner or classroom client. Your capstone must do something useful for an intended user: a reviewed report or content kit, spreadsheet-supported briefing, customer-service pack, form-and-template process, or manually operated AI-assisted service. Ordinary documents, spreadsheets, forms, presentations, and available AI assistants are enough. Controlled deployment means making a versioned artifact available to a partner or classroom test user, checking access, and recording a real user task with permitted inputs. A public launch, custom software, paid tool, real sale, or payment is not required. A mockup alone is insufficient at the end; it must connect to a usable output or service that can be tested. The five briefs provide practice and components for one capstone. The portfolio selects a feasible scope; the readiness plan and proposal define the pilot and offer; the final report and presentation demonstrate the implemented solution, deployment/user-test record, economics, operating plan, and handoff. Reuse earlier work within the existing page and slide limits. Majid Memari teaches and assesses the course. If partner access is unavailable, the instructor supplies the brief and a classroom proxy tests the solution under the same rubric. Use fictional, supplied, public non-sensitive, or specifically approved partner information, and distinguish actual classroom observations from scenario numbers. A useful starting brief might read: “Cedar Market staff repeatedly explain store policies while other customers wait. We propose investigating which questions recur, how often they require an exception, and whether clearer information would help. The decision is whether to improve the policy page, introduce reviewed AI-assisted drafts, or keep the current process. We would compare total staff effort, policy accuracy, and customer understanding.” Notice what this brief does not claim: it supplies no invented savings or demand estimate. It identifies a decision and the information needed to make it. Keep four headings in your working document—problem, alternatives, evidence, recommendation—and update them as the course progresses. A useful product or service begins with this brief, not with a list of tool features. A fictional neighborhood retailer, Cedar Market, receives repeated questions about opening hours, returns, product availability, and gift ideas. Its owner wants to “use AI” but has not identified a specific problem or a budget. The shop has a small team and a basic website. Read the syllabus, grading information, and instructor’s calendar. Review the semester partner brief or select an instructor-approved supplied case. Identify the user, need, available resources, and what a minimum usable solution could deliver. Draft a short project charter naming the problem, proposed output, data/tools available, and the decision the pilot should inform. List two assumptions you would need to investigate and identify who would use the resulting product or service. Identify a possible controlled deployment location and a partner or classroom test-user role. Public launch and actual sales are not required. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Count the problem before choosing a tool Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. An illustrative weekly question log for Cedar Market. Counts describe question types; they do not establish handling time or importance. Question type Questions in the illustrative week Returns and exchanges 16 Product availability 12 Opening hours 8 Gift ideas 4 Total 40 What share of questions concerns returns? Which two categories account for the largest combined share? Why would frequency alone be insufficient for prioritization? Returns are 16 ÷ 40 × 100 = 40%. Returns plus availability are 28 ÷ 40 = 70%. Opening-hours questions are 20% and gift questions 10%. The denominator is all 40 questions in this illustrative log, not all customers or all sales. A frequent question may be quick to answer, while a less frequent exception may take much longer or affect an important customer decision. Combine counts with time, quality, and consequence before choosing a solution."}, {"title": "Module 01: AI Literacy and Effective Use", "url": "module-01.html", "summary": "Brief a ready-made AI tool or evaluate a supplied output using explicit facts and constraints.", "text": "Brief a ready-made AI tool or evaluate a supplied output using explicit facts and constraints. Check factual claims and calculate an accuracy-style rate with the correct denominator. Explain why a headline percentage does not replace judgment about material errors. AI tools can assist with drafting, summarizing, comparing, and generating alternatives. Their fluent output can also contain unsupported claims, omissions, or inconsistent reasoning. Treat an output as a draft to evaluate, not as proof. A useful business question is whether the output helps a person perform a task to an acceptable standard, with an appropriate review burden. You do not need to understand model engineering to make that judgment. A useful request states the business task, relevant facts, intended audience, constraints, and desired format. For example: “Using only the fictional store policy below, draft a 100-word response to a customer asking about returns. If the policy does not answer a question, say what staff must confirm.” Then iterate: ask for two alternatives, compare them against your criteria, and request a targeted revision such as shorter wording or a clearer next step. Change one important instruction at a time so you can explain why the output improved. The same briefing process works when you draft manually. Longer prompts are not automatically better, and a detailed request cannot supply missing evidence. For an AI-assisted operating role, specify the input, task, output, evidence, acceptance check, and escalation rule. You might act as the coordinator of a research helper, draft helper, and review helper in separate chat sessions. These are bounded delegated tasks; they do not require autonomous software or remove your responsibility to approve the result. Judge a response against a simple checklist: Is it consistent with the supplied facts? Does it answer the customer’s actual question? Is its tone appropriate? Does it make promises the business cannot keep? Keep the original and revised outputs so you can explain your decisions. Disclose the tool and the part of the work it supported. If you use supplied outputs instead, document the same review process. For a numerical review, record both the number of supported claims and the number checked. Do not average percentages from differently sized groups unless equal group weighting is the question you intend to answer. Also identify the consequence of each important error: factual claims are not all equally consequential. Start with this fictional customer question: “I bought an unopened item 20 days ago and have the receipt. Can I return it?” A suitable task request is: “Using only the supplied Cedar Market policy, draft a concise answer. Preserve all eligibility conditions. Do not add promises about shipping, timing of payment, or exceptions. Identify any information staff must confirm.” A reviewed response is: “The policy allows returns of unopened items within 30 days with a receipt, so the situation you describe meets those listed conditions. Bring the item and receipt to store staff to confirm the return process.” The review record can be three lines: changed 60 days to 30; restored the unopened-item and receipt conditions; removed the unsupported shipping promise. This is an instructor-authored model response, not a claim that a particular AI tool produced it. You can evaluate and improve it manually. Cedar Market’s fictional policy permits returns of unopened items within 30 days with a receipt. A sample AI response says, “We accept all returns for 60 days, no questions asked, and refund shipping.” The response sounds friendly, but the owner has never approved those promises. Use this fictional customer question for the exercise: “I bought an unopened item 20 days ago and still have the receipt. Can I return it?” The policy provides no shipping-refund rule and no guarantee about the time needed to process a refund. Use the supplied customer question and return policy, or draft an equally small fictional question/policy with three relevant facts. Ask a ready-made AI tool for a draft, or use the flawed response already printed in the case; both routes provide an output to evaluate. Mark every factual statement as supported, unsupported, or uncertain. Revise the response and explain two edits that improve accuracy or usefulness. Record the tool used and how you reviewed it, or state that you used the supplied-output route. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. A percentage needs the right denominator Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. Three instructor-created reply audits, each checking a different number of factual claims. A supported claim agrees with its supplied fact source. These are not measurements of a real AI product. Illustrative reply Supported factual claims Factual claims checked Reply A 8 10 Reply B 5 5 Reply C 7 10 Total 20 25 What percentage of checked claims is supported in each reply? What is the supported-claim rate across all checked claims? Why is the simple average of the three percentages a different answer? The reply rates are 80%, 100%, and 70%. Across all claims, 20 ÷ 25 = 80% are supported. Averaging the three reply percentages gives 83.3%, because that method gives the five-claim reply the same weight as each ten-claim reply. Both calculations have a defined meaning, but they answer different questions: a randomly selected checked claim versus an equally weighted reply. State the unit and denominator. Neither statistic alone proves that a response is usable: one unsupported refund promise can matter more than several harmless correct details."}, {"title": "Module 02: Business Problems and AI Opportunities", "url": "module-02.html", "summary": "Frame a specific business problem and compare AI-assisted and non-AI alternatives.", "text": "Frame a specific business problem and compare AI-assisted and non-AI alternatives. Calculate and interpret a mean, median, and range in a small time table. Estimate workload using explicit assumptions and explain sensitivity to unusual cases. “Improve the business” is too broad to guide a solution. A usable problem statement identifies the person, task, obstacle, and consequence: “Store staff spend time repeatedly answering return-policy questions, delaying other customer service.” Separate symptoms from causes. If the policy is unclear, an accurate policy page may help more than a new AI tool. The problem should be understandable without naming a technology. For a venture, ask who experiences the problem, who can pay, what they do now, and why changing would be worthwhile. For an internal project, identify the user and budget owner. A genuine need may be studied through public information or a permitted voluntary conversation, but the supplied fictional discovery packets are sufficient for the course. Consider the status quo, a straightforward process improvement, and an AI-assisted option. A comparison should include expected benefit, effort, cost, quality risk, and staff responsibilities. AI does not need to win. Recommending a clearer form or FAQ can demonstrate strong business judgment. Avoid assuming every minute saved becomes a cash saving: time may be redirected to other work rather than removed from payroll. A baseline describes current performance; a target describes the improvement you hope to achieve. For a fictional case, you may state that staff handle 40 policy questions per week and spend four minutes on each, provided these are labeled assumptions. Explain how the business could later verify them. Separate observations supplied by the case from your estimates and from possibilities suggested by AI. The mean is the total divided by the number of observations; the median is the middle of ordered observations, averaging the two middle values when needed. The range is the maximum minus the minimum. Use a small table or dot plot to see whether a long task changes the average. A convenient average from a few cases is not automatically a representative baseline. Use a clearly labeled planning scenario. Current work is 40 questions × 4 minutes = 160 minutes per week. Suppose a clearer FAQ reduces questions by 25%, with 10 minutes of weekly upkeep: 30 × 4 + 10 = 130 minutes, releasing 30 minutes. Suppose AI-assisted drafts take one minute to prepare plus two minutes to review for each of 40 questions, with 15 minutes of upkeep: 40 × 3 + 15 = 135 minutes, releasing 25 minutes. Under these assumptions, the FAQ releases slightly more time; neither result is observed. Setup effort, tool expense, accuracy, and customer access could change the decision. A defensible recommendation is to test the simpler FAQ first and reconsider AI if routine question volume remains high. The strongest evidence to collect is the actual reduction in questions and the complete review burden—not whether a demonstration looks impressive. Assume Cedar Market receives 40 return-policy questions each week, taking four minutes of staff time per question. The owner proposes an AI assistant. A staff member suggests a clearer policy page and a prepared response template. The case does not establish which option customers would prefer. Write a one-sentence problem statement naming the affected person and business consequence. Sketch the current process in three to five plain-language steps. Compare the status quo, a simple process change, and an AI-assisted response or service concept. Estimate one benefit with visible units and labeled assumptions. Use the worked FAQ-versus-drafting scenario if useful, and include total review/upkeep effort plus a quality measure. Recommend an option, list two uncertainties, and add an AI-use disclosure. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Typical time and total workload are different questions Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. A six-request timing exercise, in minutes. These simulated values illustrate how to inspect a four-minute planning average; they do not verify the earlier 40-question baseline. Request Handling time (minutes) 1 2 2 3 3 3 4 4 5 5 6 7 Calculate the mean and median handling time. Which measure helps estimate total workload, and which describes a typical middle request? If the last request takes 19 minutes instead of 7, what changes? The total is 24 minutes; the mean is 24 ÷ 6 = 4 minutes. With six ordered values, the median is the average of the third and fourth: (3 + 4) ÷ 2 = 3.5 minutes. The range is 7 − 2 = 5 minutes. If the last value becomes 19, the total becomes 36 and the mean becomes 6, while the median remains 3.5. The mean reflects the long request’s contribution to workload; the median is less affected by it. Neither should be hidden. A dot plot or simple bar chart makes the unusual request visible. Six requests may not represent a whole week."}, {"title": "Module 03: Marketing and Sales", "url": "module-03.html", "summary": "Connect an AI-assisted marketing artifact to a customer, offer, and measurable action.", "text": "Connect an AI-assisted marketing artifact to a customer, offer, and measurable action. Compare counts, response rates, percentage-point differences, and cost per inquiry. Explain uncertainty and why a small uncontrolled comparison does not prove causation. Marketing work begins with the audience, need, value proposition, and desired next action. “Create social media posts” specifies an output but not a business objective. A more useful brief might help first-time gift buyers understand a retailer’s offer. Give an AI assistant a small fact sheet and ask for alternatives emphasizing different benefits, then select and revise the strongest one. A message can connect a feature to a customer benefit—for example, local pickup may support convenient planning—but avoid claiming an outcome you have not established. Measure success against the intended customer action rather than the number of messages generated. Turn the brief into a small acquisition plan: one audience, one channel, one useful message, and one clear next action. The next action might be a qualified inquiry or a product walkthrough, not necessarily a purchase. Create the message in class; sending or publishing it is optional. A polished message may still contain invented discounts, product features, testimonials, or guarantees. Review every factual or comparative claim against the fictional product brief. Avoid treating “best,” “guaranteed,” or “saves 50%” as harmless filler. Consistent tone, accessibility, and clear calls to action also matter. A business remains responsible for material it chooses to publish. Output counts are easy to collect but rarely establish value. Compare measures such as qualified inquiries, completed purchases, or customer comprehension with costs and review time. A small concept test can ask classmates to interpret two fictional messages. Such feedback supports a claim about that exercise, not a claim that a real market has been validated. Report the scope and limits of the evidence. Compare like denominators. A rise from 4% to 5% is one percentage point and a 25% relative increase, not a one-percent relative improvement. Show the underlying counts because a high percentage from a small group can be unstable. Without comparable groups or a suitable test design, different results may reflect audience, timing, or other factors rather than the message. A simple funnel records people reached, qualified inquiries, proposals or trials, purchases, and repeat use. Define each stage before comparing rates. Customer acquisition cost is acquisition spending divided by new customers over a stated period, not spending divided by clicks. If no customers are acquired, report that fact rather than dividing by zero or inventing a return. A grounded message is: “Make your weekend picnic easier with TrailBox: a $30 kit with reusable containers, available for local pickup. Ask about pickup arrangements.” Its claim audit is short: price, reusable containers, weekend use, and pickup come from the fictional brief; ease is a proposed benefit, not a measured result; delivery and environmental superiority are not supported. A second message could emphasize planning convenience without adding new product facts. For a classroom concept test, show both messages in a varied order and ask reviewers what the product costs, how they receive it, and what they think is included. Record misunderstandings and revise the message. Do not report a preference tally as a sales forecast. A real business would need suitable evidence about inquiries, purchases, acquisition cost, and fulfillment capacity before increasing campaign spending. A fictional service, TrailBox, sells a $30 weekend picnic kit with reusable containers and local pickup. It does not offer delivery. An AI-generated advertisement promises “free same-day delivery” and calls it “the most sustainable picnic kit in Utah,” although no comparative study exists. The owner wants a message for local customers planning a weekend outing. The brief does not specify kit contents, pickup hours, product availability, delivery, or comparative environmental evidence. Treat those details as unknown rather than filling them in. Choose a fictional product or service and specify one customer segment. Write a short acquisition or adoption brief: audience, approved facts, channel, message, and desired next action. Identify the funnel stage that action represents. Create two short messages with a ready-made AI tool or manually, or compare the supplied flawed advertisement with the worked revision. No live marketing campaign is required. Audit claims and revise both messages; include one annotated before-and-after example. Recommend one message and a simple, clearly bounded test with a success measure. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Compare rates, counts, and cost together Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. Two hypothetical TrailBox campaign scenarios. A qualified inquiry means a person asks about buying or collecting the kit. The audience sizes and spending differ, and random assignment is not established. Measure Message A scenario Message B scenario People receiving the message 100 200 Qualified inquiries 5 8 Campaign spending  $20 $30 Which message has more inquiries, and which has the higher inquiry rate? What is the percentage-point difference and relative difference in inquiry rates? Which has the lower cost per qualified inquiry, and what still prevents a firm decision? B produces more inquiries: 8 versus 5. A has the higher rate: 5 ÷ 100 = 5%, compared with 8 ÷ 200 = 4%. A’s rate is 1 percentage point higher and 25% higher relative to B’s 4% rate: (5 − 4) ÷ 4. Cost per inquiry is $20 ÷ 5 = $4 for A and $30 ÷ 8 = $3.75 for B. Therefore the higher response rate does not automatically mean the lower acquisition cost. Purchases, fulfillment cost, audience differences, and small-sample uncertainty remain unknown. These scenarios do not establish that the wording caused the difference."}, {"title": "Module 04: Customer Experience and Service", "url": "module-04.html", "summary": "Map a customer experience and design a clear human handoff.", "text": "Map a customer experience and design a clear human handoff. Calculate overall routing accuracy, missed-exception rate, and unnecessary-escalation rate from a count table. Explain how different mistakes affect customers and staff, beyond a single headline percentage. A service encounter includes what happens before, during, and after a response. Ask how the customer enters the process, what information they need, how they know the issue is resolved, and what happens if the response is wrong. AI may help draft or organize a response, while a person remains responsible for exceptions. Convenience for the business should not create more work for the customer. Delivery begins after the customer or internal sponsor accepts the offer. Define intake, preparation, approval, handoff, support, and follow-up. A useful deliverable should meet the promised scope and tell the recipient what to do next. Some questions can be answered from a clear policy; others require judgment, authorization, or information that is unavailable. A service concept should state what the assistant may answer, what it must not promise, and when a staff member takes over. A handoff is useful only if the customer knows what will happen next and the staff member receives enough context to help. Faster replies are not automatically better service. A useful evaluation includes policy accuracy, clear language, resolution, customer effort, and appropriate escalation. Consider both routine and exception cases. A storyboard or sample conversation can reveal problems before the business invests in a system. In this course, evaluating those artifacts is sufficient; a working chatbot is not required. Count missed exceptions separately from unnecessary escalations. The first uses all true exceptions as its denominator; the second uses all routine requests. Overall accuracy can look high when most requests are easy, even if a consequential minority is handled poorly. Plain-language error counts make the business tradeoff clearer than a single score. To study retention, define an eligible cohort and time window: for example, buyers with a full month in which to reorder. Repeat-purchase rate is repeat buyers divided by those eligible buyers, not repeat orders divided by every historical lead. For internal services, continued use and task completion may be more relevant than purchases. An appropriate response is: “Regular check-in begins after 3 p.m. Early check-in depends on staff confirmation, so I cannot guarantee access at 9 a.m. You can ask the front desk to confirm whether an earlier room is available.” In a proposed service storyboard, the next scene can show a request routed to the front desk with the requested arrival time, the policy question, and the fact that no promise has been made. Label that scene as a proposed process; do not say a real request was sent. The final scene shows staff confirming availability or explaining the available choices. A quality review asks whether the guest understands the uncertainty, knows the next step, and receives a consistent answer. Do not invent luggage storage, compensation, or a response-time commitment absent from the policy. A fictional hotel, Harbor Stay, permits check-in after 3 p.m. Early check-in depends on staff confirmation. A guest asks for guaranteed 9 a.m. access because of a morning flight. A draft AI reply confirms the request without checking availability. The front desk is the only role authorized in this fictional case to confirm room availability. No luggage-storage policy, compensation policy, or guaranteed response time is supplied. The storyboard may propose a handoff, but it must not imply that an actual booking or request has been made. Map a fictional customer journey in four to six steps. Identify one point where an AI-assisted response could help and one exception requiring a person. Create a three-scene storyboard or short sample conversation using only case facts. You may adapt the complete response and handoff example in the lesson. Test it against a routine question, an ambiguous question, and an exception. Revise the artifact and recommend service-quality measures and an escalation owner. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Service accuracy can hide the costly mistakes Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. A simulated routing exercise for 80 requests. An instructor’s case key identifies 60 routine requests and 20 exceptions that require staff. “Escalated” means sent to a person; it does not mean the person has already resolved the issue. Request type in the case key Kept in routine service Escalated to staff Total Routine request 54 6 60 Exception requiring staff 4 16 20 Total 58 22 80 What percentage of all requests was routed correctly? What percentage of true exceptions was missed, and what percentage of routine requests was escalated unnecessarily? Why is the overall accuracy percentage insufficient for a service decision? Correct routing is 54 routine requests plus 16 escalated exceptions: 70 ÷ 80 = 87.5%. Missed exceptions are 4 ÷ 20 = 20% of true exceptions. Unnecessary escalations are 6 ÷ 60 = 10% of routine requests. These are different denominators. A missed exception can produce a false promise or unresolved customer need; an unnecessary escalation adds staff work and delay. Both matter, but their consequences need not be equal. Calling all 58 routine-service cases “successfully automated” would conceal the four missed exceptions. Show the two error types alongside the headline rate."}, {"title": "Module 05: Business Knowledge and Decision Support", "url": "module-05.html", "summary": "Use supplied business information to create an evidence-based decision brief.", "text": "Use supplied business information to create an evidence-based decision brief. Identify unsupported conclusions and missing information in a summary. Communicate uncertainty and preserve responsibility for the decision. AI-assisted summaries can help a manager locate themes, compare documents, and prepare questions. They can also omit exceptions or present a tentative claim as certain. Keep the source material available and trace material conclusions back to it. The manager should be able to explain the recommendation without relying on the authority or confidence of the AI tool. The result can be a finished business product: a cash briefing, research summary, supplier comparison, or operational status pack. Specify the reader, decision, update frequency, source information, and review owner so it can be used consistently rather than remaining a one-off generated answer. A fact is explicitly supported by the supplied information. An interpretation explains what the facts may mean. A recommendation proposes action given an objective and tradeoffs. These are different kinds of statements. For example, a longer delivery time is a fact in a vendor table; calling a vendor “unreliable” may be an unsupported interpretation; choosing a backup supplier is a decision requiring context. A decision brief should state what is known, what is assumed, what remains unknown, and what evidence would change the recommendation. Avoid inventing a missing number to make a table look complete. If a business needs information that is unavailable, identify a practical way to obtain it. The best output may be a concise set of questions rather than an immediate answer. Assume, solely for this example, an order of 100 units. North’s listed purchase cost is $1,200; Mesa’s is $1,400, a $200 premium for a listed delivery time three days shorter. These figures exclude shipping, defects, returns, and the consequence of delay. A concise memo might say: “For routine replenishment, North is the provisional lower-price option if seven-day delivery is acceptable. For an urgent order, Mesa may be worth the premium if its delivery commitment meets the need. We cannot establish superior reliability because Mesa’s defect rate is missing and North’s reported rate lacks comparison details. Confirm total delivered price, delivery commitment, and comparable quality information before committing.” Do not automatically multiply North’s 2% rate by the order and call the result a guaranteed loss. The reported rate may not predict this order, and the cost of a defect is unspecified. A fictional retailer compares two suppliers. North Supply charges $12 per unit, delivers in seven days, and reports a 2% defect rate. Mesa Supply charges $14, delivers in four days, and has no defect-rate information in the case. A generated summary says Mesa is “proven more reliable and cheaper overall.” Use the supplied supplier facts or create a similarly small fictional business information set. Ask a ready-made AI tool to summarize the options, or evaluate the supplied “proven more reliable and cheaper overall” summary against the case facts. Label the output’s key statements as fact, interpretation, recommendation, or unsupported claim. Create a comparison table that leaves unknown values visibly unknown. Write a recommendation for a named decision-maker, including a condition that would change it. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Supplier numbers support a conditional recommendation Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. The prices, lead times, and North’s reported defect rate come from the fictional case. A 100-unit order is an additional calculation assumption. Unknown is not zero. Measure North Supply Mesa Supply Unit price $12 $14 Listed delivery time 7 days 4 days Reported defect rate 2% Unknown Illustrative order size 100 units 100 units Listed purchase cost before other charges $1,200 $1,400 How much more does the Mesa order cost in dollars and percent? How much shorter is Mesa’s listed delivery time? Can you conclude that Mesa has a lower defect cost? Mesa costs $200 more: ($1,400 − $1,200) ÷ $1,200 = 16.7% above North’s listed purchase cost. Its listed delivery time is three days shorter, or 3 ÷ 7 = 42.9% shorter relative to North. These comparisons use different units and denominators. Mesa’s defect rate and the cost per defect are unknown, so no lower defect-cost claim is supported. North’s reported 2% is not a guarantee of exactly two defects in this order. Shipping, minimum orders, return terms, and delivery reliability may change the total-cost comparison."}, {"title": "Module 06: Digital Products and AI-Enabled Service Implementation", "url": "module-06.html", "summary": "Translate the agreed business need into a minimum usable product or service.", "text": "Translate the agreed business need into a minimum usable product or service. Construct a document, spreadsheet, form/template, or manually operated AI-assisted solution with ordinary tools. Test the artifact on three supplied cases, revise it, and identify what remains before a controlled user deployment. A digital product can be a reusable document kit, a content pack, a decision aid, or a proposed app experience. An AI-enabled service can deliver a finished research brief, reviewed marketing package, or customer-response set. Describe the user, the job they need done, the useful output they receive, and what makes the output ready to use. Select one central use case. For ListingLift, the product is not merely “AI writes text”; it is a retailer receiving a clear listing with its claims checked against the product facts. The business value could come from consistency, convenience, quality, or reduced effort, and each is a hypothesis to evaluate. Choose the smallest implementation that completes a useful task. A document product can contain three reviewed pieces; a reporting kit can calculate and explain a supplied table; a service can turn an intake brief into a checked deliverable; a simple form/template process can collect inputs and guide a manual handoff. Ordinary no-code tools are sufficient. A slide prototype can help explain an interface, but the final capstone must connect it to a usable deliverable or service. Build the artifact itself and test a routine request, a missing-information case, and an exception. Record actual classroom observations and one revision without calling them market validation. A concept review can ask whether the intended user understands the offer, what problem it solves, and what concerns remain. Positive comments do not establish willingness to pay or long-term demand. A useful test has a question, a specific artifact, a limited audience, and a record of what changed. Classmate role-play or supplied case responses can substitute for external outreach. An offer card can state: “For [user], we deliver [specific useful result], including [scope], through [process], with [review/limit].” If you are exploring a venture, add a hypothetical price and next step; an internal initiative can use a resource request. Make it clear what is promised and what is not included. Use this fictional fact sheet: “Cedar ceramic mug; 12-ounce capacity; $20; dishwasher-safe; local pickup.” An unsafe draft says: “Handmade from 100% recycled materials, this unbreakable mug includes free delivery.” None of those additions is supported. A reviewed sample is: “Cedar Ceramic Mug — $20. A 12-ounce ceramic mug that is dishwasher-safe and available for local pickup. Ask the shop about current availability.” The product concept is a small, usable listing kit: an input fact sheet, a reviewed listing, and a claim-check note. Its storyboard shows the retailer supplying facts, receiving a draft, reviewing claims, and approving the final version. This example demonstrates a possible service output and review process. It does not prove that the product is automated, that customers will pay, or that it saves time. A finished document kit can be a valid course artifact even when no app exists. A fictional concept called ListingLift would help independent retailers turn a short product fact sheet into a reviewed online listing. The team has a three-slide storyboard and two sample listings, but no application. A reviewer likes the layout yet cannot tell who checks product claims before publication. For a complete supplied-output exercise, use this fictional product fact sheet: Cedar ceramic mug, 12-ounce capacity, $20, dishwasher-safe, local pickup. The flawed sample listing says: “Handmade from 100% recycled materials, this unbreakable mug includes free delivery.” Evaluate the additions against the fact sheet and produce a reviewed listing or annotated correction. Choose one fictional product or service idea connected to an earlier case brief. Write a value proposition naming the user, problem, and useful result. Implement a small usable document product, reporting kit, form/template process, or reviewed service sample. An interface mockup must connect to a usable manual output or process, not stand alone. Identify where AI assistance and human judgment would contribute. Test the artifact against three supplied or fictional use cases, record pass/fail or quality observations, and make one revision. Explain remaining uncertainty; no public launch is required. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. A concept test measures understanding, not market demand Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. Five fictional classroom reviewers interpret who approves a ListingLift output before publication. The same reviewers see the first and revised storyboard, so practice or familiarity could affect the comparison. Fictional reviewer Correctly identifies approver: first storyboard Correctly identifies approver: revised storyboard R1 Yes Yes R2 No Yes R3 Yes Yes R4 No No R5 No Yes What is the correct-interpretation rate for each storyboard? Express the change in percentage points and as a relative change. What can and cannot be concluded from five repeated classroom reviews? The first rate is 2 ÷ 5 = 40%; the revised rate is 4 ÷ 5 = 80%. The increase is 40 percentage points, or 100% relative to the original 40% rate. One reviewer represents 20 percentage points in a group of five, so the percentage is sensitive to a single response. The exercise suggests a clearer approval step for these fictional reviewers; it does not establish customer demand, willingness to pay, or causal proof that the revision alone produced the difference. Record the counts, not only the impressive-looking relative increase."}, {"title": "Module 07: Prioritizing AI Opportunities", "url": "module-07.html", "summary": "Compare multiple AI opportunities using consistent business criteria.", "text": "Compare multiple AI opportunities using consistent business criteria. Explain why a lower-complexity option may be a better first investment. Select a capstone opportunity and document the reasoning. A business rarely has enough time, money, or attention to pursue every idea. Review your five case briefs as a portfolio. Compare expected customer or operational value, confidence in the evidence, effort, risks, and organizational fit. Use the same criteria for each opportunity so an attractive presentation does not substitute for a strong business case. A simple one-to-five score can make reasoning explicit, but it is still a judgment based on limited information. Define what a high or low score means and explain the important scores. Do not hide uncertainty behind a precise total. An option with high potential value but weak evidence may need a small concept test before a larger investment. The best initial opportunity is often narrow enough to evaluate and useful enough to matter. Identify dependencies such as staff time, content quality, approval, or customer acceptance. State a reason to proceed, defer, or reject each option. Select one opportunity for the remaining capstone assignments and explain what you would need to learn before expanding it. A one-person business coordinating AI-assisted research, drafting, review support, and customer follow-up is one possible operating scenario. It is not a forecast that all businesses will work this way or that staff effort disappears. Check which roles still consume the owner’s time and which tasks should remain manual before proposing expansion. Suppose you use four criteria scored from 1 to 5, with higher always better: business value 30%, ease of a small pilot 25%, evidence confidence 25%, and manageability of risk 20%. These weights are an illustrative management choice. A product-listing concept scored 4, 4, 3, 4 totals 3.75: (4 × .30) + (4 × .25) + (3 × .25) + (4 × .20). A response-drafting concept scored 5, 3, 2, 2 totals 3.15. The numbers are judgments, not measurements. Explain why evidence confidence or risk scores differ. If later evidence raises the response concept’s confidence to 5 and risk manageability to 4, its score becomes 4.30 and the ranking changes. That reversal shows which information matters before a commitment. Use the matrix to organize a discussion; a small numerical lead is not an objective verdict. Cedar Market is considering an AI-assisted product-description service, customer-response drafts, supplier-comparison summaries, a gift-finding concept, and a staff scheduling idea. The owner has limited review time. The scheduling idea could affect employees but has the weakest information and the most unresolved concerns. Reuse your five case briefs and update any conclusion that changed after feedback. Define four or five comparison criteria and make score direction explicit. Use the worked matrix as a model, but explain the judgments for your own five opportunities. Create a one-page comparison matrix, explain material assumptions, and vary the most uncertain score or weight once to see whether the preferred option changes. Select one capstone opportunity and identify a practical first step. Add a short reflection on why two other ideas should be deferred or rejected. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Weighted priorities make judgments visible Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. Illustrative scores from 1 to 5, with higher always better. Weights are business value 30%, pilot ease 25%, evidence confidence 25%, and risk manageability 20%. Scores are management judgments, not observed performance. Opportunity Value (30%) Pilot ease (25%) Confidence (25%) Risk manageable (20%) Product-listing kit 4 4 3 4 Response drafts 5 3 2 2 Supplier summaries 3 4 3 4 Gift-finding concept 3 3 2 3 Staff scheduling concept 5 2 1 1 Calculate the weighted score of the listing kit and response drafts. Which opportunity ranks first under these assumptions? What happens if evidence raises response drafts’ confidence to 5 and risk manageability to 4? The listing kit scores 4×.30 + 4×.25 + 3×.25 + 4×.20 = 3.75. Response drafts score 3.15; supplier summaries 3.45; gift-finding 2.75; scheduling 2.45. The listing kit ranks first under these judgments. Raising the response option’s confidence and risk scores as stated produces 4.30 and changes the ranking. A small bar chart can communicate the original scores, but its caption must identify them as weighted judgments. The sensitivity result tells you which evidence could change the decision; it does not make either ranking objective."}, {"title": "Module 08: Responsible AI and Vendor Evaluation", "url": "module-08.html", "summary": "Identify material business risks and assign responsibility for handling them.", "text": "Identify material business risks and assign responsibility for handling them. Compare solution options using business needs and verifiable vendor information. Explain what should remain under human review in the chosen concept. Consider who could be affected by inaccurate content, unfair treatment, disclosure of information, or an inappropriate promise. A risk statement should name the possible failure, who is affected, and its consequence. Then identify an owner, preventive step, review point, and response. “Use AI responsibly” is an intention; a named reviewer and a clear escalation rule are operational decisions. Compare options using the needs of your use case: output quality, ease of use, accessibility, data handling, review controls, support, price structure, and exit options. Do not equate a well-known brand with suitability. For classroom analysis, use supplied fictional vendor profiles or publicly available information. Label unavailable information as unknown, and avoid presenting marketing language as independently established performance. For course activities, use fictional or instructor-supplied information and avoid confidential records. In a business proposal, identify what information the service would actually need and what should be excluded. Ask how access, retention, review, and vendor changes would be handled. A business decision-maker should know which questions require institutional, contractual, legal, or specialist review before real adoption. A concise purchasing note might say: “Tool B’s review and export features match our needs, but we have not established retention terms or accuracy for our use case. Test both options with the same fictional fact sheets and request authoritative answers to the missing terms. Retain a manual option until the requirements are met.” Turn that into a small risk register: unsupported product claim → store manager checks each claim against the fact sheet → pause if an unapproved claim reaches the final output; unavailable service → staff use the approved template → record the interruption; inability to retrieve work → confirm export with sample documents before relying on the tool. Each entry names a failure, owner, preventive action, and response. A feature list is not a control unless the business can use it consistently. Price, reputation, and a persuasive demo do not settle suitability. Two fictional tools support product-description drafting. Tool A costs less but its supplied profile does not explain retention or export options. Tool B costs more and includes role-based review and document export. Neither profile provides independent evidence of output accuracy. For comparison practice only, assume Tool A costs $18 per month and Tool B $42 per month; these are invented vendor profiles, not actual prices or recommendations. Both can draft a listing from supplied product facts. Tool B’s profile states that work can be exported as a document and a reviewer can approve drafts. Actual quality, the usefulness of these features, and retention terms remain unverified for both. A manual template is the third option. Identify three requirements for your capstone concept and two unacceptable outcomes. Compare the two supplied fictional vendor profiles and the manual alternative, or use two documented public options. Mark a stated feature separately from evidence that it works for your need. List three material risks and assign an owner, control, and response to each. Mark vendor claims, verified information, and unknowns separately. Carry the comparison into next week’s readiness plan. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. A cheaper subscription can require more staff effort Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. A separate fictional comparison using the same 12 practice fact sheets. “Pass first review” means all factual checks pass before correction. Mean total time includes preparation, review, and correction. Prices are invented teaching assumptions, not actual vendor quotes. Option Drafts passing first review / 12 Mean total minutes per completed listing Monthly subscription assumption Tool A 9 / 12 9 $18 Tool B 11 / 12 7 $42 Manual template 12 / 12 12 $0 Compare first-review pass rates without treating 12 examples as proof of reliability. At an assumed 40 listings per month, how much staff time does B release relative to A? If staff capacity is valued at $24 per hour, does B’s higher price necessarily make it worse? First-review pass rates are 75% for A, 91.7% for B, and 100% for the manual template in this fictional exercise. Twelve examples are a small, constructed set; these are not general vendor accuracy claims. B takes two fewer minutes per listing than A, or 80 minutes at 40 listings monthly. That capacity is valued at 80 ÷ 60 × $24 = $32, compared with a $24 subscription premium. The illustrative capacity-value difference is $8 in B’s favor before other costs. It is not cash savings if payroll stays fixed. Quality, retention, access, and exit requirements still matter."}, {"title": "Module 09: Pilot Deployment and Adoption Planning", "url": "module-09.html", "summary": "Design a small pilot that tests a specific business assumption.", "text": "Design a small pilot that tests a specific business assumption. Define success measures, review responsibilities, and stop conditions. Identify the staff support and process changes needed for adoption. A useful pilot has a narrow business question, a defined user and task, a versioned usable solution, an access/delivery location, a time limit, and a decision it will inform. The Week 9 assignment is the readiness plan; the controlled deployment and user-test record are completed before the final capstone submissions. For example, a user can open a reviewed listing kit, complete a product-listing task, and explain what required correction. The pilot can occur privately in class with supplied fictional inputs. It must be a real test of the artifact, not invented results or only a proposal for future testing. Include the time to prepare inputs, review outputs, correct mistakes, and handle exceptions. Compare the pilot with a baseline rather than with an imagined perfect manual process. Define a quality measure alongside speed or cost. Fictional numbers may illustrate the calculation, but they must be labeled assumptions. A plan is not a completed experiment; do not invent results. A solution can be easy to demonstrate and difficult to adopt. Identify who does the work, who approves it, what training or guidance they need, and how concerns are handled. A small pilot should have a clear owner and a way to revert to the prior process. Staff feedback can reveal extra work or hidden dependencies that a polished concept misses. Draw a plain-language operating sequence: intake → AI-assisted preparation → human check → delivery → follow-up. For each handoff, name the owner, required evidence, and escalation condition. This operating playbook can be run manually with ordinary tools; an agent platform or integration is unnecessary. A bounded illustrative protocol is: “Compare both methods on the same 20 fictional product fact sheets. Alternate which method is used first to reduce simple practice effects. Record input preparation, drafting, checking, correction, and final factual accuracy. Keep the scope to product listings and have a designated reviewer approve each result.” A proposed decision rule might require at least 20% lower median total preparation time and no unsupported product claims in approved outputs. Those thresholds are classroom planning choices, not industry standards. Pause the exercise if review is bypassed or confidential information is introduced. A measurement log can have five columns: case, method, total minutes, factual corrections, and approval outcome. Leave results blank until work is actually performed. The Week 9 submission requires this plan; before the final capstone, execute the agreed smaller controlled user test and record its actual outcome. The illustrative 20-case comparison is a planning example, not a mandatory sample size. Never invent results to make a plan look complete. Cedar Market plans to compare manual and AI-assisted drafts for 20 fictional product descriptions. The owner proposes measuring drafting time only. A staff member points out that product claims must still be checked and some drafts require extensive revisions. State one hypothesis for your chosen capstone opportunity and the decision it will inform. Define a controlled deployment using the semester’s permitted data and tools. Specify the usable version, access/delivery location, test-user role, and task; use the classroom sandbox if external use is unavailable. Specify the baseline, full-process effort, quality measure, review method, and proposed continue/revise/stop thresholds. Explain why the thresholds fit your business case. Assign responsibilities and describe staff guidance, escalation, and fallback. Add a compact intake–prepare–review–deliver–follow-up operating sequence with any bounded AI-assisted roles. Include the vendor/risk comparison and a short deployment log template. Leave outcome fields blank until the user test occurs, then reuse the completed record in the final capstone. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Measure the complete process and the quality boundary Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. Eight paired, simulated timing rows for arithmetic practice; these are not executed results from the proposed 20-case pilot. Each time includes all preparation and checking. The final column indicates an unsupported claim remaining in the approved assisted output. Practice case Manual total minutes Assisted total minutes Unsupported claim in approved assisted output? 1 8 6 No 2 10 7 No 3 12 10 No 4 14 9 No 5 9 8 No 6 11 10 No 7 13 12 No 8 15 18 Yes Compare total time, mean time, and median time for the two methods. Does the scenario meet a proposed 20% total-time reduction threshold? How should the remaining unsupported claim affect a rule requiring none in approved output? Manual time totals 92 minutes and assisted time 80, a 12-minute or 12 ÷ 92 = 13.0% reduction. Means are 11.5 and 10 minutes. Medians are 11.5 and 9.5 minutes. The long assisted case affects the mean more than the median. The 20% reduction threshold is not met, and one approved output violates the zero-unsupported-claim quality rule. The appropriate conclusion is to revise or pause under those proposed rules, not to declare success from faster typical cases. Eight simulated rows also do not complete the proposed 20-case learning plan."}, {"title": "Module 10: Business Models and Competitive Strategy", "url": "module-10.html", "summary": "Explain how the proposed solution creates value and who benefits or pays.", "text": "Explain how the proposed solution creates value and who benefits or pays. Compare alternative business models or internal funding rationales. Identify a credible basis for differentiation beyond access to an AI tool. A solution creates value when it helps someone achieve a useful outcome. A commercial offering captures some of that value through a price or revenue model. An internal business project may instead justify its cost through better service, avoided losses, or increased capacity. State whether your capstone is an internal improvement or an external product/service so its economic logic is clear. An offer must also specify what the buyer receives, how much support or revision is included, and what happens next. Treat a proposed price or resource request as a hypothesis until supported. A classroom offer card is not a binding agreement or evidence of a sale. Describe the customer, problem, value proposition, delivery approach, costs, benefits or revenue, and success measures. These elements should agree. A low-price service that requires extensive personal review may be difficult to sustain. A premium service needs a defensible reason customers would pay more. A one-page business-model canvas is useful when its claims are specific and its assumptions are visible. Using the same AI tool as competitors is not, by itself, a durable advantage. A business may distinguish itself through a focused customer need, trusted relationships, useful packaging, reliable review, convenience, or a better service experience. Explain how the offer fits into the customer’s existing work: where they find it, what they supply, what they receive, and why switching is worthwhile. The user may not be the buyer, so identify who approves payment or staff effort. Compare existing alternatives, including templates and doing nothing. A promising concept still needs a plausible route to customers and a way to deliver consistent value. Consider explicitly hypothetical figures for a reviewed listing service: eight minutes of staff work at $24 per hour costs $3.20 per listing. Add an assumed $0.80 allowance for other variable delivery costs, giving $4 per listing before fixed costs and customer acquisition. At $8 per listing, contribution is $4 per listing; that is not net profit. A $99 subscription including 25 listings produces only $3.96 per listing at full use, slightly below the assumed $4 variable cost. At ten listings of use, it leaves $59 before fixed and acquisition costs. This difference shows why usage, review effort, and support expectations matter. Do not choose a price solely because it sounds affordable. Explain who pays, what they receive, what work delivery requires, and what assumptions would make the offer sustainable. ListingLift could be sold as a monthly subscription, a per-listing service, or an internal tool used by a retailer’s staff. The concept still requires a person to check every product fact. The founder has not decided whether customers value speed, consistency, or hands-on review most. The user and buyer may differ: an employee prepares listings while an owner approves spending. The current alternative is a reusable template plus staff review. Any proposed price, usage volume, or customer commitment must be labeled as an assumption unless evidence is actually supplied. State whether your capstone is an internal initiative or an external offering. Complete a one-page model covering user, buyer or budget owner, problem, value, delivery, alternatives, economics, and measures. For an internal project, explain who authorizes the effort even when there is no sales price. Compare two ways to deliver, price, or fund the offer. State what is included, how customers or internal users find it, and how usage, human review, and support affect cost. Identify three assumptions that materially affect viability. Prepare questions for next week’s concept validation exercise. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Subscription usage changes contribution Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. Hypothetical ListingLift subscription: $99 monthly revenue and $4 variable delivery cost per listing. Variable cost includes an assumed $3.20 staff-time valuation plus $0.80 other delivery costs. Fixed costs, acquisition, and taxes are excluded. Listings delivered in the month Subscription revenue Variable delivery cost Contribution before excluded costs 10 $99 $40 $59 20 $99 $80 $19 25 $99 $100 −$1 Why does the same subscription price produce different contribution? At full use of 25 listings, what are revenue and variable cost per listing? What usage threshold should be investigated before promising 25 included listings? At 25 listings, revenue per listing is $99 ÷ 25 = $3.96, below the assumed $4 variable cost. Contribution is $99 − $100 = −$1 before fixed or acquisition costs. At ten listings, contribution is $59 because fewer units consume delivery effort. The mathematical zero-contribution point is $99 ÷ $4 = 24.75 listings, so 24 whole listings remain above zero and 25 below it under the assumptions. This is contribution, not net profit or necessarily cash margin when staff time is valued rather than newly paid. A sustainable offer must account for usage variation and excluded costs."}, {"title": "Module 11: Customer Discovery and Concept Validation", "url": "module-11.html", "summary": "Ask neutral discovery questions and interpret feedback within its evidence limits.", "text": "Ask neutral discovery questions and interpret feedback within its evidence limits. Calculate simple response rates and explain small-sample sensitivity and sampling bias. Revise a tangible business concept without treating simulated interest as validated demand. Questions such as “Would you love an AI assistant?” invite agreeable but weak evidence. Ask how the person handles the task now, what is difficult, what consequences follow, and what alternatives they have tried. Avoid leading language and promises. In this course, classmate role-play or instructor-supplied case responses are sufficient; real customer outreach is not mandatory. After discovery, practice a short sales or internal-approval conversation: confirm the problem, ask about fit and constraints, show the artifact, explain scope and price or effort, respond to one objection, and agree on a next step. Do not force a close when the fit is weak. Role-play with the supplied responses is enough. A role-play response, a fictional case, and a real interview are different evidence sources. Label each accurately. Record both favorable and unfavorable feedback and separate what the participant said from your interpretation. Three positive comments do not establish a market. Useful evidence may show that the problem is smaller than expected, the proposed experience is confusing, or a simpler solution is preferable. Always show a response count and denominator beside a percentage. One response moves a five-person group by 20 percentage points; a larger group is less sensitive to one response but can still be biased by recruitment or leading wording. Interest, an intention to buy, and an actual purchase are different outcomes. No formal statistical test is required to recognize these limits. Use a storyboard, mockup, sample output, or manual service walkthrough to give feedback something specific to address. Ask what the reviewer thinks the product does, what is useful, what is unclear, and what would prevent adoption. Document one meaningful change and why it follows from the evidence. Use this feedback to strengthen the proposal and improve the usable business artifact; a custom application is unnecessary. Keep discovery and selling distinct: questions should first help you understand the problem; a later proposal presents a specific offer. A positive role-play reaction is not a commitment. Record what objection changed the offer and what evidence would justify a real launch or purchase. Refine the usable version and the planned deployment from this feedback. Discovery evidence can remain simulated, but the final user test must truthfully record someone using the implemented artifact in the approved partner or classroom setting. An evidence table can use four columns: response, challenged assumption, implication, next question. For supplied Retailer A, “checking takes longer than drafting” challenges drafting as the main bottleneck; the next question is what makes checking difficult. B’s existing templates challenge differentiation; ask what those templates fail to provide. C’s seasonal interest suggests a narrower use occasion, while the concern about claims reinforces the need for review; ask what an acceptable review would look like. A justified revision is a reviewed seasonal listing kit with a visible claim-check record, compared against the retailer’s existing template. The conclusion is “the concept is more specific and its assumptions are clearer,” not “three customers validated demand.” You may use this complete supplied-case route. For a different business, label any adapted responses as fictional scenario material and explain which assumptions they help you examine. For ListingLift, three supplied fictional responses are available: Retailer A says checking product facts takes longer than drafting text; Retailer B likes consistent tone but already uses templates; Retailer C would consider a reviewed service for seasonal products but worries about inaccurate claims. None states a verified willingness to pay. Write five neutral discovery questions tied to the assumptions in your business model. Use three clearly labeled role-play responses or the three supplied ListingLift responses. For another business, you may adapt them as explicitly fictional scenario material; real outreach is optional. Summarize each response, the assumption it challenges, the implication, and the next question in a short evidence table. Preserve contradictions and evidence limits. Revise the artifact and business model after one material finding. Rehearse a short offer conversation addressing one objection and a proportionate next step; label it as role-play. Combine the problem, evidence, concept, business model, and next validation step into a concise proposal. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Small samples and different questions require different interpretations Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. All three rows are invented evidence scenarios for interpretation practice, not real outreach and not additional student requirements. “Positive” means expressed interest; “completed” means a hypothetical purchase choice. These endpoints cannot be treated as interchangeable. Illustrative evidence scenario Count with stated outcome People in scenario Outcome being counted Classmate role-play 4 5 Expressed interest Target-customer interview scenario 28 40 Expressed interest Purchase-choice scenario 3 20 Completed a purchase choice Calculate each rate and state exactly what it measures. How much does the interest rate change if one positive response becomes negative in the first two rows? Why can none of these fictional rows establish actual customer demand? The rates are 80% expressed interest, 70% expressed interest, and 15% purchase choices. Changing one positive response changes the first rate by 1 ÷ 5 = 20 percentage points, but the second by 1 ÷ 40 = 2.5 points. The larger denominator is less sensitive to one response; it does not automatically make sampling representative or remove leading-question bias. Interest and a purchase choice measure different behavior. All rows are fictional, so they are practice in statistical interpretation, not evidence about a real market. For real data, record recruitment, question wording, context, and limitations before generalizing."}, {"title": "Module 12: Economics and Business Value", "url": "module-12.html", "summary": "Calculate costs, capacity gains, and simple value estimates with visible units and periods.", "text": "Calculate costs, capacity gains, and simple value estimates with visible units and periods. Distinguish contribution, assumed economic value, and realized cash return. Use a scenario table and threshold to explain uncertainty in the recommendation. A business case includes more than a tool subscription. Consider setup effort, staff review, training, recurring use, support, and correction of mistakes. For a product or service, include the work needed to acquire and serve customers. Use a simple table and arithmetic rather than a complicated model. Label fictional figures and identify which assumptions have the greatest effect on the result. For an external offer, distinguish acquisition spending, variable delivery cost, and fixed operating costs. Contribution per order is price minus variable delivery cost; it must cover fixed costs and any excluded effort before there is profit. A repeat-customer assumption can change the economics, but a small fictional cohort does not justify a confident lifetime-value forecast. Time saved can create capacity without reducing cash expenses. Revenue is the money received; profit depends on costs. Avoid counting the same benefit twice, such as both labor savings and extra output from the same hours. If benefits are difficult to monetize, state a relevant quality or service measure alongside the financial estimate. Do not force every improvement into an unsupported dollar figure. A base estimate is only one possible outcome. Compare a lower-benefit or higher-cost case with your base case and explain what changes. Break-even can be expressed as the volume or benefit needed to cover costs. A recommendation becomes stronger when it states the conditions under which it would no longer make sense. Precision in arithmetic does not make uncertain assumptions certain. A return ratio is generally net benefit divided by the relevant investment or cost, with a clearly stated period and included items. If benefits are only assigned values for released staff capacity, label the ratio accordingly; it is not realized cash ROI. A simple table separating recurring cash changes, capacity value, and one-time effort is more informative than an impressive unlabeled percentage. With 100 listings monthly, manual work is 100 × 12 ÷ 60 = 20 hours. AI-assisted drafting plus five-minute review is 100 × (3 + 5) ÷ 60 = 13.33 hours. The scenario releases 6.67 hours, valued at $160 using the assumed $24 hourly rate. Subtracting the $60 tool expense gives $100 of illustrative net capacity value, not demonstrated cash profit: payroll is unchanged and the business must use the released time productively. Setup is four hours, valued at $96, shown separately as a one-time effort. If review takes eight minutes, the process uses 18.33 hours; released capacity is worth only $40, below the $60 expense. Under the stated recurring assumptions, capacity value equals tool cost at 7.5 minutes of review. This threshold helps identify the measurement that matters; it is not a promise of savings. Assume a service prepares 100 product listings per month. Manual work takes 12 minutes each; AI-assisted drafting takes three minutes plus five minutes of review. Staff time is valued at a fictional $24 per hour, and the tool costs $60 per month. Setup takes four staff hours. No staff hours are removed from payroll. List one-time and recurring costs for your capstone using labeled assumptions. Calculate a benefit or revenue estimate with a visible time period, units, and arithmetic. Use the worked listing-service case if helpful, then explain your own chosen assumptions. Explain whether time gains create capacity, cash savings, or another benefit. Compare a base case with a less favorable case. Identify a break-even or decision threshold and state whether it concerns cash, contribution, or the value of released capacity. State which economic assumptions need verification. Continue the agreed controlled deployment and record any actual task-time/quality observations separately from the supplied scenario figures. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Economic value is not the same as realized cash Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. The same 100-listing monthly scenario used in the lesson. Payroll remains unchanged and no additional sales are supplied. Setup uses four staff hours valued at $24 per hour; no extra cash wage payment is assumed. Quantity Base: 5-minute review Less favorable: 8-minute review Manual preparation time 20 hours/month 20 hours/month Assisted preparation time 13.33 hours/month 18.33 hours/month Capacity released 6.67 hours/month 1.67 hours/month Capacity valued at assumed $24/hour $160/month $40/month Tool subscription cash expense $60/month $60/month Capacity value less tool expense $100/month −$20/month Known recurring cash change under stated assumptions −$60/month −$60/month One-time setup effort valuation $96 $96 Verify the released hours from 100 listings and the per-listing times. Why are the capacity-value result and cash-change result different? What is the danger in labeling ($160 − $60) ÷ $60 as demonstrated cash ROI? The base calculation is 100×[12−(3+5)]÷60 = 6.67 released hours, valued at $160. The stress case releases 1.67 hours, valued at $40. Existing payroll does not fall, so known cash expense increases by $60 in both scenarios; the released time might create future value, but none is demonstrated. ($160−$60)÷$60 = 166.7% is a return ratio based on assumed capacity value and subscription spending only, not proven cash ROI. It excludes setup and other possible costs. Show a benefit-minus-cost calculation with its period and assumptions, and separately identify actual changes in cash receipts and payments."}, {"title": "Module 13: Executive Communication", "url": "module-13.html", "summary": "Present a clear recommendation supported by selected business evidence.", "text": "Present a clear recommendation supported by selected business evidence. Use a concept artifact to explain a product or service without technical detail. Respond to questions about assumptions, risks, and alternatives. An executive audience needs to know what decision you recommend and why it matters. Open with the business problem, your recommendation, and the expected value. Then provide the evidence necessary to assess it. A chronological account of every activity is usually less useful than a clear argument. Explain uncertainty directly rather than burying it in an appendix. A customer-facing pitch and an executive recommendation use different emphases. A customer needs the useful outcome, scope, price, trust, and next action. An internal sponsor needs value, resources, risk, and an approval decision. Your final presentation may address either route while making the whole business logic assessable. Use six to eight slides with a clear purpose for each: problem and customer, proposed experience, evidence, business model, economics, risks/adoption, and recommendation. A storyboard or sample output can make the concept concrete. Keep labels readable and distinguish illustrative material from observed results. A software demonstration is not required; the audience should understand the business proposition through the artifact. Questions reveal whether your reasoning is complete. Be ready to explain why you chose this option, what evidence supports it, what could go wrong, and what would change your mind. When you do not know, identify the missing information and how you would obtain it. Confident delivery does not require pretending uncertainty has been resolved. A useful opening is: “I recommend a limited comparison of reviewed AI-assisted product listings with our current template process. The business question is whether total preparation effort can fall without unsupported product claims. The main uncertainty is review time, so I am asking for a bounded learning exercise rather than a full launch.” A closing decision slide can name the owner, 20 fictional comparison cases, measures, stop conditions, and a decision date. If both methods are applied to all 20 cases, the earlier 12-minute manual and eight-minute assisted assumptions imply about 6.67 staff hours before planning and analysis; do not promise the pilot can be completed in one hour. A planning allowance of roughly eight staff hours would itself remain an estimate. Keep the hypothetical business request distinct from student course requirements: no paid purchase or external public deployment is required; the capstone still includes its agreed controlled classroom or partner deployment. A student opens a presentation with five minutes of tool features and finishes by asking for a $2,000 pilot. The audience still does not know the customer problem, the pilot’s success measure, or who will review outputs. The $2,000 figure is the student’s unsupported request, not an established budget or vendor quote. The class should identify what scope, cost basis, responsibility, and decision rule would be needed before the audience could assess it. Draft six to eight slides using the proposal and economics table. Include the usable artifact, route to users/customers, offer or approval request, and delivery/improvement plan. Include the implemented artifact and an early deployment/user-test record. State where it was made available, who tested it by role, what task occurred, and what changed. Practice a seven-minute presentation that ends with a specific decision request. Use instructor/peer feedback or self-review to identify two revisions. Check that the requested pilot scope, time allowance, measures, and responsibilities fit together. Prepare answers to three likely questions about evidence, economics, or risk. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Choose a figure that supports the decision Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. Use the lesson’s fictional base scenario to prepare an executive visual. The figures are estimates under assumptions, not measured results. Time and money have different units and should not share an unlabeled scale. Measure Current process Proposed base scenario Staff preparation time 20 hours/month 13.33 hours/month Tool subscription expense $0/month $60/month Realized payroll reduction supplied by case $0/month $0/month Observed customer-value evidence Not supplied Not supplied What chart would show the time comparison clearly? What title or caption would prevent the chart from implying observed cash savings? Which accompanying number or limitation must remain visible? A two-bar chart can compare estimated monthly staff hours, using a zero baseline and a clearly labeled hours axis. A suitable title is “Estimated preparation time under base assumptions,” with a note that figures are fictional and include review. The decrease is 6.67 hours, about 33.3% of 20 hours. Show the $60 monthly tool expense separately and state that payroll reduction and additional customer value have not been established. A truncated axis or a title such as “Proven savings” would overstate the evidence. The figure should make a specific decision easier to assess, not merely decorate the deck."}, {"title": "Module 14: Pilot Evidence, Revision, and Handoff", "url": "module-14.html", "summary": "Interpret the actual controlled user test without overstating market or financial results.", "text": "Interpret the actual controlled user test without overstating market or financial results. Revise the implemented product or service and reconcile its evidence, economics, and operating plan. Prepare a usable handoff and a supported continue, revise, or stop recommendation. A coherent recommendation connects the problem, proposed experience, evidence, economics, and adoption plan. Check that the customer and use case are consistent across documents. A proposal for a reviewed premium service should not use the costs of an unreviewed automated product. Make sure your measures assess the value you claim and that the pilot can realistically inform the decision. Also reconcile acquisition, selling, and delivery: an inexpensive offer cannot quietly depend on unlimited personal support, and a growth plan must fit capacity. Check that the sample artifact delivers the promised value and that a person remains accountable for every AI-assisted task. Visual polish helps communication, but unsupported demand claims, omitted costs, and unclear responsibilities are more consequential weaknesses. Rank revisions by their effect on the decision. Address factual gaps and contradictions first, then improve structure and readability. Reuse earlier material thoughtfully, removing claims that later evidence no longer supports. A strong final report shows what was implemented, how it was made available, what the test user actually did, what feedback or measurements were collected, and what changed. Fictional inputs are legitimate; invented test events are not. Distinguish a successful classroom task from proof of customer demand, revenue, or production reliability. A limited pilot can reveal that expansion should stop even when the minimum usable solution was completed. Provide the final artifact, short instructions, source/assumption record, known limitations, owner, and support/closeout plan. No continuing support commitment should be implied without agreement. Use three columns: claim, supporting artifact or calculation, and needed revision. Example: “fully self-service” conflicts with the storyboard’s reviewer; revise to “reviewed service concept.” “Saves cash” conflicts with unchanged staffing; revise to “releases capacity under stated assumptions,” unless you can support an actual spending reduction. “Customers want this” relies only on supplied fictional responses; revise to “the classroom exercise identified assumptions to test.” “Lower cost” relies on drafting time alone; include review, correction, and ongoing support. These changes make the recommendation more credible without adding another project. Finish with a one-paragraph revision note: what changed, what evidence or inconsistency caused the change, and how the recommendation is now different. Preserve justified uncertainty rather than filling every gap with an invented number. A capstone report claims a fully self-service product, but its storyboard shows a staff member reviewing every output. The economics omit that staff time. The pilot measures user satisfaction but the recommendation promises cost savings. Audit the report, deck, artifact, and figures for consistency across need, offer, acquisition/adoption, selling/approval, delivery, and economics. Use the worked claim–evidence–revision audit. Complete the agreed controlled user test if not already done. Record the artifact version, access check, user role, task, actual result, feedback, and any measured quality or time. Revise the artifact and calculations in response to the evidence. Keep supplied hypothetical data separate from actual classroom or partner test observations. Assemble the short handoff: final artifact/location, instructions, worked example, review checklist, known limitations, owner, and continue/revise/stop decision. Confirm that final submissions meet the existing assignment requirements and course deadlines. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. Audit the evidence behind numerical claims Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. A fictional review of 12 material claims in a capstone draft. “Supported” means traceable to supplied facts or a cited source for the stated claim; “assumption” means explicitly labeled; “unsupported” means neither. This is a writing audit, not a measure of product success. Claim status Number of material claims Supported 7 Clearly labeled assumption 3 Unsupported 2 Total 12 What share of claims is supported and what share remains unsupported? If the two unsupported claims are removed, what is the supported share of the remaining claims? Why does the higher percentage not mean new evidence was collected? Supported claims are 7 ÷ 12 = 58.3%; unsupported claims are 2 ÷ 12 = 16.7%. Removing the unsupported claims leaves ten claims, of which 7 ÷ 10 = 70% are supported and 30% are labeled assumptions. The higher percentage comes from changing the denominator, not from collecting evidence. Removing an unnecessary unsupported claim can improve honesty, but a material uncertainty may instead need to remain visible as an assumption or open question. Report both counts and definitions so a percentage does not conceal what changed."}, {"title": "Module 15: Capstone Demonstrations, Pitch, and Handoff", "url": "module-15.html", "summary": "Demonstrate a usable AI-assisted business solution and its controlled deployment/user-test evidence.", "text": "Demonstrate a usable AI-assisted business solution and its controlled deployment/user-test evidence. Explain the need, customer or sponsor, acquisition/adoption, selling/approval, delivery, economics, and limits. Defend a proportionate next decision and hand off the artifact with clear ownership and support boundaries. The final capstone connects a business need to an implemented solution, controlled deployment, user-test evidence, numerical analysis, operating plan, and sales or adoption recommendation. Demonstrate what the user can actually do and explain the role of AI assistance and human review. Your conclusion may be to continue, revise, or stop expansion; the grade depends on the quality of implementation, evidence, reasoning, and communication, not sales revenue or enthusiasm for AI. Internal finance, marketing, MIS, and operations solutions are as valid as a venture. No actual public launch, commercial transaction, or external partner is required. Show the final usable document product, reporting kit, form/template process, customer-service pack, or manually operated AI-assisted service. Explain where the controlled version was delivered or shared and show the user-test record. A screenshot or storyboard can help explain the experience, but a mockup or pitch deck alone is insufficient. Custom code, a public application, and a live technical demonstration are unnecessary; the agreed no-code business task must have been implemented and tried by a partner or classroom user. During Q&A, connect answers to the implemented artifact, actual pilot observations, supplied scenario analysis, and remaining assumptions. Submit a consistent report, deck, artifact, deployment/test record, and concise handoff through the existing assignments. State which outcomes were observed and which remain hypotheses. Explain who owns future use, corrections, maintenance, and access, or why the classroom pilot ends here. A clear stop recommendation is legitimate after honest implementation and testing. A final decision record can state: “We implemented a reviewed listing kit and shared version 2 with a classroom test user. The user completed the agreed listing task using supplied fictional facts; the record identifies the actual corrections and revision. We recommend a limited next step because demand and the economic assumptions remain unverified.” Replace those example details with what actually occurred. Name the decision, evidence, uncertainty, owner, and stop condition. Do not claim a deployed application, real customer sale, or realized savings unless those events actually happened and are appropriate to disclose. A controlled classroom deployment proves only what its task and evidence support. A fictional decision-maker has reviewed your proposal and asks: “What exactly are you asking us to approve, what would we learn, and what would make you stop?” The decision-maker is willing to fund a small learning exercise but does not want unsupported promises. Finalize the 4–6-page business report, excluding a short appendix. Submit the six- to eight-slide deck, implemented artifact, deployment/user-test record, and concise handoff to the existing presentation assignment; keep the report within 4–6 pages. Deliver a seven-minute presentation followed by approximately three minutes of instructor/class Q&A, according to class arrangements. Demonstrate the usable solution, summarize the actual controlled test, and state the sales/adoption recommendation, key uncertainty, and condition that would change it. Complete the final discussion by reflecting on a decision that changed during the course. Use this module’s data table to calculate and interpret the requested quantities. Keep counts, denominators, units, and uncertainty visible; incorporate the relevant table or small figure into the existing artifact rather than creating another submission. A decision scorecard can recommend revision Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. This scorecard interprets the eight-row simulated exercise from Module 9 against the proposed pilot rules. It does not represent an executed pilot or actual business results. Decision criterion Proposed rule Fictional exercise result Interpretation Total preparation-time reduction At least 20% 13.0% Below threshold Unsupported claims in approved outputs 0 1 Quality rule violated Completed comparison cases in the proposed plan 20 8 simulated practice rows Plan not completed Which rules are met, and which are not? What recommendation follows if quality is a condition of proceeding? How can the next-step request be specific without claiming the pilot succeeded? Neither the time rule nor the quality rule is met, and the eight invented practice rows are not a completed 20-case pilot. A defensible classroom recommendation is to revise the review process and test the consequential assumption before considering expansion. State the owner, the limited next comparison, the measures, and the conditions that would change the decision. Faster performance on some rows is useful to inspect but does not override a required quality boundary. The numbers support reasoning about a hypothetical decision, not a claim that an actual business should launch."}, {"title": "Module 16: Reflection and Next Steps", "url": "module-16.html", "summary": "Identify transferable business skills developed through the course.", "text": "Identify transferable business skills developed through the course. Select a useful next step for a concept without overstating its readiness. Organize completed work into a clear personal learning record. The course’s core process applies beyond any particular AI tool: define a problem, compare alternatives, examine evidence, make the concept understandable, estimate value, and plan responsible adoption. Tools will change, but the need for clear judgment remains. Review your work for the parts of that process that became stronger. A completed capstone includes an implemented business artifact or service and a controlled user test; it is not automatically a validated market or a production-ready application. A next step may be an appropriately approved partner pilot, further customer discovery, improved reliability, or closing the classroom project. Match the next step to the uncertainty and resources that matter. This optional reflection adds no new requirement. If you use the project in a portfolio, label fictional data, classroom role-play, illustrative artifacts, and AI assistance. Describe your own analysis and decisions clearly. A concise account of what you learned and changed is more credible than claiming a launched business or implemented system that does not exist. An accurate portfolio description could be: “Implemented a reviewed retail-listing kit, delivered a version to a classroom test user, documented a task using fictional product data, and revised the kit from the feedback. Produced scenario economics and a handoff plan.” Describe the actual delivery method, role of AI, your own contribution, and limits. Do not turn a private classroom deployment into a claim of commercial launch, real customer validation, or realized savings. The optional reflection creates no new graded work, meeting, submission, or deadline. A student wants to describe a capstone professionally. The student built a reviewed document product, made it available to a classmate in a private classroom pilot, recorded a task using fictional inputs, and revised the artifact. No public application was built and no real customers were enrolled. Collect the final report, deck, implemented artifact, deployment/user-test record, and handoff in your own working folder. Write three lessons you want to carry into future business decisions. Label the evidence and maturity of the concept accurately. Choose one optional next learning step or explain why you would stop here. Transfer the method, then recheck the denominator Fictional teaching data, not real customer, vendor, or pilot observations. Optional arithmetic reflection only. Compare two fictional contexts with the same four-minute capacity gain per task, $24 hourly staff-time valuation, and $60 monthly tool expense. Payroll is unchanged in both. Quantity Retail listing context Lower-volume service context Tasks per month 100 20 Minutes released per task 4 4 Capacity released per month 6.67 hours 1.33 hours Capacity valued at $24/hour $160 $32 Monthly tool expense $60 $60 Capacity value less tool expense $100 −$28 Why does the recommendation change even though the per-task improvement is the same? What is the recurring volume threshold for capacity value to equal the $60 expense? Which assumptions need checking before transferring a claim to another business? At 20 tasks, 20×4÷60×$24 = $32 of assumed capacity value, below the $60 expense. At 100 tasks it is $160. Each task releases capacity valued at $1.60, so the threshold is $60÷$1.60 = 37.5 tasks; 38 whole tasks exceed it under the assumptions. This is not a cash break-even claim because payroll remains unchanged. Recheck volume, actual time saved, review quality, staff use of the released capacity, and costs in the new context. This optional table adds no submission, grade, meeting, or deadline."}, {"title": "Business Case Brief 1: Problem and Opportunity", "url": "assessment-01.html", "summary": "Assessment 01 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Identify a concrete business problem and decide whether AI assistance is a sensible option. Write 1–2 pages; focus on the decision rather than tool setup. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A specific problem statement naming the affected customer or employee and the business consequence. A simple current-process sketch and comparison of the status quo, a non-AI improvement, and an AI-assisted concept. One compact quantitative table with units, period, denominators where relevant, and labeled assumptions; interpret a mean/median or benefit estimate, a quality measure, and two uncertainties. A small dot plot or bar chart may clarify variation. Include review/upkeep effort and distinguish capacity from cash savings. A short evidence and AI-use disclosure."}, {"title": "Business Case Brief 2: Marketing and Sales", "url": "assessment-02.html", "summary": "Assessment 02 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Evaluate how AI assistance could improve a specific marketing or sales task. Write 1–2 pages and include a small sample message or similar artifact. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A customer or internal-user segment, offer, channel, communication objective, and desired next action. Show where the action belongs in a simple acquisition or adoption funnel. Two short message alternatives or one annotated before-and-after example. You may manually revise the supplied TrailBox advertisement; no paid tool or actual campaign is required. A claim-level review of factual support, tone, brand fit, and limitations, with unsupported promises removed or visibly identified. A recommendation supported by a compact count/rate/cost table using supplied fictional campaign data or labeled assumptions. Show percentage-point versus relative changes correctly, identify uncertainty, and propose a bounded comparison; include evidence/AI disclosure."}, {"title": "Business Case Brief 3: Customer Experience and Service", "url": "assessment-03.html", "summary": "Assessment 03 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Design and evaluate an AI-assisted customer-service experience. Write 1–2 pages with a short conversation excerpt or three-scene storyboard. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A customer or internal-user journey covering the need, acceptance or approval, delivery, support, and a useful point for AI assistance. A sample conversation or storyboard covering a routine request and an exception. A clear human-review/escalation trigger, the responsible role, the customer’s next step, and what information the handoff conveys. Distinguish the proposed process from an action actually performed. A compact quality table or figure using the supplied routing counts or a similarly labeled scenario. Explain missed exceptions and unnecessary escalations with their correct denominators, then make a service recommendation and disclose evidence/AI use."}, {"title": "Business Case Brief 4: Knowledge and Decision Support", "url": "assessment-04.html", "summary": "Assessment 04 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Use a small fictional information set to prepare a reliable business recommendation. Write 1–2 pages; technical knowledge systems are not part of this assignment. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A named business decision and a small numerical comparison table showing units, prices or rates, and unknown values. Use the supplied supplier case if desired. An evaluation of a supplied or AI-assisted summary, distinguishing supported claims from unsupported conclusions. A recommendation with explicit dollar/percentage comparisons where supported, alternatives, missing information, and a condition that would change the decision. Preserve unknown values rather than replacing them with unsupported estimates. A concise evidence trail and AI-use disclosure."}, {"title": "Business Case Brief 5: First Usable Product or Service", "url": "assessment-05.html", "summary": "Assessment 05 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Implement the first usable version of the scoped business solution. Write 1–2 pages plus a small document product, reporting kit, form/template process, or reviewed service. A mockup can explain the interface but must connect to a usable output or process. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A concise offer card naming the user, problem, useful result, included scope, and relevant price or internal resource assumption. A first usable document product, spreadsheet-supported briefing, form/template process, or reviewed service sample. A prototype interface must connect to a manual output or process that completes the agreed task. An explanation of where AI assistance and human judgment contribute to the experience. A three-case test-and-revision record for the implemented artifact, using supplied or permitted inputs. Record actual classroom checks, one revision, and readiness for the later controlled user deployment. Keep provided statistical scenarios separate from observed test results."}, {"title": "AI Opportunity Portfolio and Prioritization", "url": "assessment-06.html", "summary": "Assessment 06 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Synthesize and improve your five case briefs, then select one opportunity for the remaining capstone work. Submit a 3–4-page synthesis with your existing briefs or artifacts as an appendix; do not create five new projects. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A concise summary of the five opportunities and material revisions after feedback. A weighted comparison table with four or five defined criteria, consistent score direction, and explained numerical judgments. Show the calculation for one option and vary one uncertain score or weight; note whether the ranking changes. A small score chart may support the same discussion. A selected capstone opportunity, reasons for the choice, and a practical first step. Reasons to defer or reject at least two alternatives, plus a reflection and evidence/AI disclosure."}, {"title": "Pilot Deployment and Readiness Plan", "url": "assessment-07.html", "summary": "Assessment 07 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Plan the controlled deployment of the implemented capstone. Submit 2–3 pages naming the user, usable version, delivery/access location, task, approval route, measures, and fallback. This milestone is the plan; execute the agreed partner or classroom user test before the final submissions. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A specific business question, minimum usable version, partner or classroom test-user role, deployment/access location, task, baseline, and decision the pilot informs. A measurement table with units and denominators, covering business value, quality, complete staff workload, and numerical continue/revise/stop thresholds. Identify what would be recorded and how averages or rates would be interpreted. Leave result cells blank unless observations were actually collected; supplied simulated data must remain labeled. A comparison of two solution options and a manual alternative, distinguishing vendor-stated features, tested evidence, and unknowns. The supplied fictional profiles are sufficient; no purchase or real system test is required. A short risk register and operating sequence from intake through preparation, human review, delivery, and follow-up. Name bounded AI-assisted roles, owners, evidence checks, escalation, and fallback; no agent software is required. A short deployment/test log template and source/AI-use disclosure. Include version/date, delivery method, access check, user task, actual outcome, feedback, revision, and limits; leave future results blank."}, {"title": "Capstone Business Proposal and Pilot Agreement", "url": "assessment-08.html", "summary": "Assessment 08 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Develop your selected opportunity into a coherent business proposal. Submit 3–4 pages plus a small concept artifact and concise evidence table. Reuse and revise earlier work. Real customer outreach is optional; three clearly labeled classmate role-play responses or the supplied fictional case responses fully satisfy the discovery requirement. For another business, adapted responses must be labeled fictional scenario material, not real interviews. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A specific customer or internal stakeholder, problem, current alternative, and value proposition. A one-page business model covering user, buyer or budget owner, offer/scope, route to customers or internal users, price or funding, delivery, support, costs, and key assumptions. This replaces a generic feature list; it is not an extra document. Five neutral discovery questions and a concise table of three role-play or supplied-case responses. Identify challenged assumptions, implications, and evidence limits. Include one small quantitative table of supplied concept-test counts or business assumptions, showing denominators and why the numbers do not establish real demand. The refined implemented artifact or service, plus a short agreed pilot scope: user role, location/delivery method, permitted inputs, task, measures, owner, and partner/instructor approval before the controlled test. A material evidence-based revision, a short sales or approval conversation addressing one objection and next step, and the next validation question. Role-play is sufficient; disclose sources, simulations, and AI assistance."}, {"title": "Final Capstone Report: Implementation, Evidence, and Value", "url": "assessment-09.html", "summary": "Assessment 09 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Prepare a 4–6-page executive report, excluding a short appendix, documenting the implemented solution, controlled deployment and actual user test, and a recommendation about its next use. Reuse revised work from the portfolio, readiness plan, and proposal. A well-supported recommendation to narrow, defer, or stop expansion can earn full credit after the agreed usable solution and controlled test are completed. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. An executive recommendation, specific business problem, intended user, and comparison with alternatives. A concise account of discovery evidence, the implemented solution, and the controlled deployment/user test. Include version, delivery/access method, user role, task, actual outcome, feedback, revision, and limits; place the short record in the appendix if needed. A simple table of one-time and recurring costs, benefits or revenue, and calculations using labeled assumptions, units, and a time period. Include one clear chart comparing the base and less favorable scenarios. Distinguish capacity gains from cash savings and revenue from profit. A base case and a less favorable scenario, with a numerical break-even or decision threshold explained. Name the quantity calculated—cash flow, contribution, or capacity value—and keep one-time setup separate. If you report an ROI-style percentage, define its numerator, denominator, period, and whether benefits are realized cash or assumed values. A concise operating and improvement plan: acquisition or adoption, selling or approval, intake, AI-assisted tasks, human review, delivery, support/retention, ownership, capacity, measures, and stop conditions. Keep it within the existing 4–6 pages. A brief handoff and learning record: final artifact/location, user instructions, review checklist, owner/support limits, what changed and why, and source/AI-use disclosure."}, {"title": "Capstone Demonstration, Business Pitch, and Handoff", "url": "assessment-10.html", "summary": "Assessment 10 · full instructions and rubric", "text": "Demonstrate the implemented capstone and pitch its adoption or sale to the instructor/class and an available partner. Submit six to eight slides, the usable artifact, a concise controlled-deployment/user-test record, and handoff material. Present for seven minutes with approximately three minutes of Q&A. No custom software or public launch is required, but a mockup alone is insufficient. Use fictional, instructor-supplied, or public non-sensitive business information. Real outreach, public launch, and actual payments are optional and are not required for the course. Use available ready-made AI or supplied AI outputs with ordinary no-code business tools; no programming, paid subscription, custom application, or public software launch is required. The final capstone requires a usable artifact/service and controlled partner or classroom deployment with an actual user-test record. A manually operated solution is valid. Label facts, assumptions, case evidence, role-play, and AI-generated material accurately. Include a brief disclosure of any AI assistance and how you checked it. Use the instructor’s calendar for your live offering. A six- to eight-slide deck connecting customer/problem, usable solution, evidence, acquisition/adoption, selling/approval, delivery, economics, and next decision. Include at least one readable numerical table or figure with units, baseline, assumptions, and uncertainty. The usable no-code business product or service, the completed controlled-deployment/user-test record, and concise handoff materials. Demonstrate the actual task and explain how AI assistance and human review contribute. A specific decision request, key uncertainty, and next step. A presentation and Q&A grounded in your own understanding, with concise evidence and AI-use disclosure in the deck."}, {"title": "The AI Fluency Framework (one-page 4D guide)", "url": "readings.html#ai_fluency", "summary": "Free reading · Free one-page PDF · CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 · Dakan, Feller, and Anthropic.", "text": "Use four connected habits: decide what to delegate, describe the task, evaluate the result, and take responsibility for its use. Keep this one-page guide beside your work."}, {"title": "Explore the business value of generative AI solutions", "url": "readings.html#business_value", "summary": "Free reading · Free Microsoft Learn reading · no paid software exercise required. Vendor examples should be evaluated critically.", "text": "Connect AI capabilities with business opportunities, readiness, costs, and responsible use. Read selected sections when assigned; completing the entire learning path is optional."}, {"title": "How generative AI can boost highly skilled workers' productivity", "url": "readings.html#jagged_frontier", "summary": "Free reading · Free MIT Sloan article · October 19, 2023.", "text": "Study why AI can improve one business task while weakening another. Distinguish a persuasive output from a correct recommendation. The findings describe the studied tasks and model, not a universal performance guarantee."}, {"title": "Generative AI at Work — abstract and introduction", "url": "readings.html#customer_service_evidence", "summary": "Free reading · Free author-posted paper · revised November 2024; published in Quarterly Journal of Economics, 2025. Only the abstract is required.", "text": "Read the abstract of a field study involving 5,172 support agents. Identify the measured outcome and differences across workers. Do not treat the study’s average productivity gain as a forecast for your own idea."}, {"title": "Build & Run Prototypes", "url": "readings.html#prototype_methods", "summary": "Free reading · Free IDEO.org method page · optional worksheet downloads.", "text": "Make an idea tangible using a storyboard, role-play, or mock-up. Decide what you need to learn, show the concept to others, and revise it from feedback."}, {"title": "How to select the next best test from the Experiment Library", "url": "readings.html#experiment_selection", "summary": "Free reading · Free article by David J. Bland · August 27, 2020. Linked commercial products are optional.", "text": "Match a small test to the riskiest business assumption. Look for useful evidence before investing heavily in a product."}, {"title": "NIST AI RMF Playbook — overview and selected business controls", "url": "readings.html#responsible_ai", "summary": "Free reading · Free NIST resource · based on AI RMF 1.0. Check the source for updates.", "text": "Use the overview page to connect risks with accountable people, controls, measurement, and a response. This is voluntary guidance, not a legal compliance guarantee."}, {"title": "10 levers for shaping generative AI that truly improves worker performance", "url": "readings.html#adoption_outcomes", "summary": "Free reading · Free MIT Sloan article · September 3, 2026 · approximately six minutes.", "text": "Look beyond speed: consider quality, rework, learning, teamwork, and accountability. Use evidence to decide whether to scale a pilot. This article summarizes interview-based research, not a universal ROI estimate."}, {"title": "Introductory Business Statistics 2e", "url": "readings.html#business-statistics", "summary": "Free quantitative companion · selected OpenStax explanations", "text": "business statistics data tables figures mean median counts proportions rates percentage points uncertainty sample population confidence interval"}, {"title": "Venture studio: from problem to first customer", "url": "venture-studio.html", "summary": "A complete worked business, AI-assisted roles, funnel, economics, and launch plan", "text": "demand customer acquisition sales selling marketing pricing contribution margin onboarding delivery retention agents human review business launch service product"}, {"title": "Business partner challenge", "url": "partner-challenge.html", "summary": "Discover, build, test, implement, deliver, and pitch a no-code solution", "text": "partner real problem demand deployment implementation handoff usable product service no code"}, {"title": "Build a Weekly Cash-Briefing Kit", "url": "case-finance-cash-brief.html", "summary": "Finance — complete application case", "text": "Cedar Services is a fictional small business whose owner receives a cash schedule but struggles to identify timing problems before making routine operating commitments. A finance assistant spends time turning the schedule into an understandable briefing. This pathway can be an internal finance improvement or a hypothetical reviewed reporting service; it does not involve investment recommendations, credit decisions, tax advice, or movement of money. This illustrative alternative uses the same milestones as the semester capstone. The confirmed brief and available resources determine the final scope. Complete a controlled partner or classroom deployment of the usable kit and record an actual user task. Help the owner understand projected cash timing, the assumptions behind it, and the questions requiring attention. A clear numerical schedule and source-checked narrative are more useful than a confident AI summary that confuses cash with profit."}, {"title": "Build and Sell a Reviewed Content Starter Kit", "url": "case-marketing-content-service.html", "summary": "Marketing — complete application case", "text": "BrightLocal Studio is a fictional small operator helping local service firms explain a specific offer clearly. The starting product is a reviewed content starter kit, not a promise of increased revenue. The course artifact can serve an internal marketing team or a hypothetical external customer. This illustrative alternative uses the same milestones as the semester capstone. The confirmed brief and available resources determine the final scope. Complete a controlled partner or classroom deployment of the usable kit and record an actual user task. Small firms need accurate, usable campaign materials tied to a customer action, but generic AI copy can invent claims and fail to connect audience, channel, offer, and delivery."}, {"title": "Design an AI-Assisted Proposal Service", "url": "case-mba-proposal-venture.html", "summary": "MBA / Strategy — complete application case", "text": "EventQuote is a fictional small operator preparing reviewed proposal packs for independent event-service providers. The scenario explores how one person might coordinate AI-assisted research, drafting, checking, and follow-up. It is a possible operating design, not a prediction that autonomous agents will run a business successfully. This illustrative alternative uses the same milestones as the semester capstone. The confirmed brief and available resources determine the final scope. Complete a controlled partner or classroom deployment of the usable kit and record an actual user task. Service providers lose time turning incomplete client requests into understandable, consistent proposals. Faster text generation alone does not solve missing requirements, unrealistic promises, or delivery capacity."}, {"title": "Build an Order-Exception Service Kit", "url": "case-mis-order-exceptions.html", "summary": "MIS — complete application case", "text": "Northline Supply is a fictional wholesaler whose staff must distinguish routine order-status questions from exceptions needing review. The proposed MIS solution is a usable information and service kit that can be run manually. Operations students can use the same case to study service quality, workload, and handoffs. This illustrative alternative uses the same milestones as the semester capstone. The confirmed brief and available resources determine the final scope. Complete a controlled partner or classroom deployment of the usable kit and record an actual user task. Inconsistent triage and incomplete handoffs cause rework and inaccurate customer promises. A high overall routing score can hide costly missed exceptions."}, {"title": "Finance & accounting — business pathway", "url": "business-paths.html#finance", "summary": "Does the proposed improvement create enough value to justify its full cost?", "text": "Financial analyst · controller · business partner Use AI to organize supplied financial information, explain a variance, or draft a management brief. Check the arithmetic, assumptions, and sources before a person acts. A monthly management-report kit: a verified variance table, a concise narrative, a sensitivity exhibit, and a human review checklist. Alternatively, design a scenario-planning service with one finished sample report. Which costs recur, and which occur only once? How does the recommendation change with volume, review time, or adoption? Is the benefit released capacity, avoided expense, or an actual cash inflow?"}, {"title": "Marketing & customer experience — business pathway", "url": "business-paths.html#marketing", "summary": "Does the message or experience improve a meaningful customer outcome?", "text": "Marketing analyst · brand manager · customer-experience lead Create reviewed campaign content, customer information, or a service experience. Separate a persuasive message from evidence of comprehension, conversion, and repeat use. A campaign product kit: a defined audience, three reviewed content pieces, a test plan, and a results brief using supplied numbers. Alternatively, create a customer-insight service with a finished sample and repeatable review process. What is the conversion denominator, and are the audiences comparable? Is the change a percentage-point difference or a relative percentage? Does a higher response rate offset acquisition, fulfillment, and review costs?"}, {"title": "MBA, management & strategy — business pathway", "url": "business-paths.html#strategy", "summary": "Which opportunity deserves limited attention, budget, and a bounded pilot?", "text": "General manager · consultant · business-unit leader Compare competing uses of AI, define a viable business model, and make an executive recommendation. Keep the scorecard, evidence, economics, and ownership connected. An executive decision pack: a prioritized opportunity portfolio, a tangible sample of the selected solution, a pilot charter, and a board-ready decision brief. Show what evidence would make you revise or stop. Do the rankings depend on a fragile weight or assumption? What is the opportunity cost of choosing one pilot over another? Which measure would justify expansion, revision, or stopping?"}, {"title": "MIS & business analysis — business pathway", "url": "business-paths.html#mis", "summary": "Will the proposed information service produce dependable results within the business process?", "text": "Business analyst · product owner · information-systems manager Translate user needs into an understandable service or decision-support concept. Specify the information needed, human review, exception handling, vendor terms, and acceptance evidence. A decision-support service package: a source-to-claim evidence table, a reviewed sample report, a storyboard of the user experience, and an acceptance checklist. Deliver the usable report service or no-code experience in a controlled pilot, evaluate its use, and prepare the handoff. A production software application is optional. What share of cases is correct, and which errors matter most? How do review effort and exception volume affect total service cost? What does a vendor claim measure, and which cases were excluded?"}, {"title": "Operations & service management — business pathway", "url": "business-paths.html#operations", "summary": "Does the complete process become more useful, reliable, and economical?", "text": "Operations analyst · service manager · process-improvement lead Improve a repetitive task, service handoff, or supplier decision. Compare the current process, a simpler improvement, and AI assistance using the same measures. A service improvement kit: an approved response set or operating guide, a human escalation map, a before-and-after workload table, and a measured pilot plan. Does the mean hide long waits or a small group of difficult cases? Does faster drafting still save time after review and correction? Which quality or safety measure must remain above the pilot threshold?"}, {"title": "Business case brief — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-business-brief", "summary": "Frame the decision before choosing a tool. Supports the five short business briefs.", "text": "BUSINESS CASE BRIEF\nBuilding AI Solutions · Majid Memari\n\nWorking title:\nAuthor:\nBusiness context (fictional, supplied, or public):\n\n1. THE PROBLEM\nWho experiences the problem?\nWhat happens today, and what business consequence follows?\nWhat observable outcome would count as improvement?\n\n2. THREE OPTIONS\nStatus quo:\nA reasonable non-AI improvement:\nAn AI-assisted product or service:\nWhy is the proposed option a better fit for this particular problem?\n\n3. THE ARTIFACT\nAttach one useful sample: a reviewed response, content item, evidence table,\nstoryboard, template, or service output.\nWho would use it, and what would they do next?\n\n4. VALUE AND EVIDENCE\nBenefit estimate (label assumptions and units):\nQuality measure:\nSource or supplied fact supporting the estimate:\nMost important uncertainty:\n\n5. RISK AND RESPONSIBILITY\nWhat could go wrong?\nWho checks or approves the output?\nWhen should a person take over?\n\n6. RECOMMENDATION\nProceed, revise, test, or stop?\nWhat is the smallest next step that could change your decision?\n\nAI disclosure: tool, purpose, representative prompt, checks, and revisions.\nUse the assignment's rubric and length requirements before submitting.\n"}, {"title": "Opportunity comparison — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-opportunity-portfolio", "summary": "Compare five ideas using the same criteria and show how uncertainty affects the ranking.", "text": "AI OPPORTUNITY COMPARISON\n\nDecision-maker:\nAvailable budget or other constraint:\n\nDefine the criteria before scoring:\n- Business value: what measurable benefit matters?\n- Feasibility: what resources, information, and skills are available?\n- Evidence: what is known, and what is assumed?\n- Risk: who could be harmed, and what controls are needed?\n\nFor each of your five existing briefs, fill in:\nOpportunity name:\nProblem and user:\nProposed artifact or service:\nValue score (1–5) and reason:\nFeasibility score (1–5) and reason:\nEvidence confidence (low/medium/high) and reason:\nMaterial risk and control:\n\nComparison rules:\nState any weights you use and why. Do not treat uncertain inputs as exact.\n\nPriority choice:\nStrongest alternative:\nTradeoff you are accepting:\nAssumption most likely to change the ranking:\nResult under a less favorable version of that assumption:\nEvidence you would collect next:\n\nAttach the five existing briefs; do not create five unrelated new projects.\n"}, {"title": "Digital-product concept card — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-concept-storyboard", "summary": "Turn a business idea into a visible user experience without building software.", "text": "DIGITAL-PRODUCT CONCEPT CARD\n\nProduct or service name:\nIntended user:\nSituation in which the user needs help:\nOne-sentence value proposition:\n\nChoose an artifact:\n[ ] Usable digital product kit\n[ ] AI-assisted service with a finished sample\n[ ] Proposed app or service shown in a storyboard or slide mock-up\n\nTHE USER'S EXPERIENCE — FOUR PANELS\nPanel 1 · Trigger: What brings the user here?\nPanel 2 · Input: What information does the user provide?\nPanel 3 · Useful result: What exactly does the user receive?\nPanel 4 · Review or next step: What does the user do, and when does a person help?\n\nWrite sample text or sketch each panel. Include at least one realistic output.\n\nBOUNDARIES\nWhat will this concept do?\nWhat will it not promise?\nWhat information may it use?\nWho reviews errors or exceptions?\n\nVALIDATION\nWhat do you need to learn from showing this concept?\nWhat task will a participant attempt?\nWhat will you observe?\nWhat result would make you revise or stop?\nLabel classroom role-play or synthetic feedback as simulation.\n"}, {"title": "Evidence and validation log — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-evidence-ledger", "summary": "Keep facts, assumptions, AI suggestions, and observed feedback separate.", "text": "EVIDENCE AND VALIDATION LOG\n\nDecision or claim being tested:\n\nFor each important claim, record:\nClaim:\nType: supplied fact / public source / assumption / AI suggestion / observation\nSource and date:\nWhat the source actually supports:\nWhat it does not establish:\nConfidence and reason:\nWhat could disconfirm the claim:\n\nSMALL CONCEPT TEST\nRiskiest assumption:\nTest question:\nParticipant type: real voluntary feedback / classmate role-play / simulated\nArtifact shown:\nTask or question (avoid leading participants):\nSuccess threshold, chosen before seeing results:\nWhat you observed (separate observations from interpretation):\nUnexpected result:\nLimitations of this evidence:\nDecision: proceed / revise / gather more evidence / stop\nNext action:\n\nDo not present AI-generated personas or simulated interviews as real demand.\n"}, {"title": "Pilot and readiness scorecard — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-pilot-scorecard", "summary": "Define a bounded business experiment with owners, a baseline, and stop conditions.", "text": "PILOT AND READINESS SCORECARD\n\nBusiness problem and proposed change:\nPilot owner:\nHuman reviewer or escalation owner:\nPilot scope and duration:\nWho is affected?\n\nBASELINE AND SUCCESS\nOutcome measure:\nCurrent baseline, source, and period:\nTarget and why it matters:\nQuality measure and minimum acceptable standard:\nReview time and correction effort to track:\nNon-AI comparison:\n\nREADINESS\nInformation needed and permission to use it:\nTraining or support needed:\nVendor claims that still need verification:\nEstimated setup and ongoing costs:\n\nRISK REGISTER — REPEAT FOR EACH MATERIAL RISK\nRisk and person affected:\nControl or safeguard:\nResponsible owner:\nEvidence the control is working:\nEscalation trigger:\n\nDECISION RULE\nExpand if:\nRevise if:\nStop immediately if:\nDecision date and decision-maker:\nHow the business returns to its previous process if needed:\n\nKeep the pilot small enough to learn safely and large enough to inform the decision.\n"}, {"title": "Business value worksheet — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-economics", "summary": "Calculate complete costs and distinguish time capacity from cash savings.", "text": "BUSINESS VALUE WORKSHEET\n\nTime period (for example, per month):\nBusiness outcome:\n\nTIME AND CAPACITY\nNumber of tasks in the period:\nBaseline minutes per task:\nMinutes per task after AI assistance, including review and corrections:\nMinutes saved per task = baseline minus assisted time:\nHours of capacity released = tasks × minutes saved ÷ 60:\nIllustrative hourly value:\nEstimated capacity value = released hours × hourly value:\n\nA capacity estimate is not automatically cash savings. Explain how the business\nwould use the released time, and whether any spending would actually fall.\n\nOTHER BENEFITS\nRevenue or quality benefit (avoid counting the same benefit twice):\nEvidence or clearly labeled assumption:\n\nFULL COSTS\nOne-time setup and training:\nRecurring tool or service fees:\nOngoing review/support cost not already included above:\nOther costs:\n\nCOMPARISON\nEstimated recurring benefit:\nRecurring cost:\nEstimated net value = recurring benefit minus recurring cost:\nIf recurring net value is positive, simple payback = setup cost ÷ net value:\nIf it is zero or negative, do not claim a positive payback period.\n\nSENSITIVITY — BASE / LESS FAVORABLE / MORE FAVORABLE\nTask volume:\nReview effort:\nAdoption:\nResulting net value:\nAssumption that matters most:\n\nRecommendation and what must be verified before spending money:\n"}, {"title": "Executive presentation outline — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-executive-presentation", "summary": "Make the decision request clear and support it with the evidence that matters.", "text": "EXECUTIVE PRESENTATION OUTLINE\n\nAudience and decision needed:\n\n1. RECOMMENDATION\nWhat should the business do, and what approval or next step are you requesting?\n\n2. PROBLEM AND USER\nWho is affected, and what evidence shows the problem matters?\n\n3. CONCEPT IN USE\nShow the implemented artifact or service and the controlled pilot result.\nUse a storyboard to explain the experience, not as the only final deliverable.\nExplain the customer's or employee's experience in plain language.\n\n4. ALTERNATIVES AND EVIDENCE\nWhat alternatives did you compare?\nWhat feedback or test supports the choice?\nWhat is still an assumption?\n\n5. ECONOMICS AND RISKS\nShow the key benefit and cost assumptions.\nExplain one less favorable scenario.\nName the human review responsibility and the most important risk control.\n\n6. NEXT STEP\nPropose a bounded pilot or validation step with an owner, measure, and stop condition.\n\nPREPARE FOR Q&A\nWhich claim is weakest?\nWhat evidence would change your recommendation?\nWhat will you say if asked for a fact you have not verified?\n\nUse the assessment's required format and length. Keep supporting detail in an appendix.\n"}, {"title": "AI task brief and review record — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-ai-review", "summary": "Give a clear instruction, inspect the output, and document your own judgment.", "text": "AI TASK BRIEF AND REVIEW RECORD\n\nBEFORE USING AI\nBusiness task:\nIntended audience:\nSupplied facts or original sources:\nConstraints (policy, tone, length, budget, privacy):\nRequired output format:\nWhat should the tool do if evidence is missing?\n\nREUSABLE PROMPT\nUsing only the information below, help me [task] for [audience].\nRelevant facts: [facts and sources].\nConstraints: [constraints].\nReturn [format]. Identify assumptions, unsupported claims, and missing evidence.\nDo not invent facts, customer feedback, prices, or citations.\n\nAFTER RECEIVING THE DRAFT\nTool and date, or supplied-output/manual route:\nUseful part of the output:\nUnsupported or incorrect claim:\nCheck against the original evidence:\nCalculation checked manually:\nRevision you made and why:\nHuman approval or escalation needed:\n\nDISCLOSURE FOR YOUR SUBMISSION\nI used [tool / supplied output] to help with [purpose].\nI checked [facts, calculations, sources] and changed [specific revisions].\nThe final recommendation and its limitations are my responsibility.\n"}, {"title": "Table and chart interpretation — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-data-interpretation", "summary": "Turn business numbers into a careful recommendation. Check the denominator, comparison, uncertainty, and chart scale.", "text": "TABLE AND CHART INTERPRETATION\n\nBusiness question and decision-maker:\nTable or chart title:\nSource and date:\nAre these observed facts, supplied fictional data, estimates, or assumptions?\n\n1. WHAT WAS COUNTED?\nUnit of observation (customer, task, order, business, or other):\nTime period and population of interest:\nSample size and how the cases were selected:\nMissing values, excluded cases, or mixed groups:\n\n2. COUNTS AND RATES\nOutcome count:\nDenominator (eligible cases or opportunities):\nRate = outcome count / denominator:\nDoes the comparison use the same definition and time period?\n\n3. A TYPICAL CASE AND THE FULL WORKLOAD\nMean (total / number of cases):\nMedian (middle value after sorting):\nExtreme values that affect the mean:\nWhich summary answers this business question, and why?\nWhat could an average hide about delays, failures, or affected people?\n\n4. DESCRIBE CHANGE PRECISELY\nBefore rate and after rate:\nPercentage-point change = after percentage minus before percentage:\nRelative percentage change = (after - before) / before x 100:\nState the baseline. Relative change is undefined when the baseline is zero.\nCould differences in case mix, timing, selection, or other changes explain the result?\n\n5. READ THE FIGURE\nHorizontal and vertical axis labels, units, and ranges:\nDoes the axis start at zero? If not, does the display exaggerate a difference?\nDo the chart and table tell the same numerical story?\nWhat uncertainty or important group is missing from the picture?\n\n6. INTERPRET UNCERTAINTY\nHow well does the sample represent the people or tasks in the decision?\nWhat plausible values or alternative assumptions could change the conclusion?\nIf an interval is supplied, what estimate and population does it describe?\nDo not treat a sample result or an interval as proof of causation.\n\n7. RECOMMEND AND CHECK\nOne sentence describing the result with numbers and units:\nWhat the numbers support:\nWhat they do not establish:\nRecommended next step and the evidence that could change it:\n\nUse ordinary arithmetic, a calculator, or the business statistics studio.\nInterpretation matters more than formula memorization.\n"}, {"title": "From problem to first customer — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-venture-launch", "summary": "Connect demand, offer, product, economics, customer acquisition, sales, and delivery in one launch plan.", "text": "FROM PROBLEM TO FIRST CUSTOMER\n\nImplement a usable artifact or repeatable service, deliver it in a controlled\npilot, evaluate its use, and practice a sales or adoption pitch. A public launch,\nreal outreach, payment, or paid tool is optional. Label classroom evidence,\nrole-play, and assumptions clearly.\n\n1. DEMAND\nSpecific customer and recurring problem:\nCurrent workaround and its cost:\nEvidence you actually have:\nAssumption that needs a customer test:\nQuestion you can ask without selling the answer:\n\n2. OFFER\nFor [customer] who struggles with [problem], we deliver [specific outcome].\nIncluded deliverables, turnaround, boundaries, and exclusions:\nWhy choose this over the current workaround?\n\n3. SOLUTION AND SAMPLE\nShow the implemented output, product kit, or repeatable service:\nControlled delivery and user test:\nA storyboard may explain the experience, but is not the only final artifact:\nRequired input and source facts:\nWhat AI assists with:\nWhat a person checks and approves:\nAcceptance criteria and exception route:\n\n4. ECONOMICS\nPrice per order:\nDirect cash cost per order:\nOwner time per order and explicit hourly assumption:\nContribution before monthly fixed costs and acquisition effort:\nMonthly fixed costs:\nCapacity at the promised service level:\nLess favorable case and what would change your decision:\n\n5. FIND CUSTOMERS\nOne appropriate channel and why this audience uses it:\nUseful message or sample that earns attention:\nProspects -> responses -> qualified conversations -> paid orders:\nCount, denominator, and assumed conversion for each stage:\nTime and cash needed for the acquisition activity:\nDo not describe a forecast funnel as observed demand.\n\n6. SELL A BOUNDED OFFER\nOpening question about the existing problem:\nShort explanation of the deliverable and boundaries:\nClear price and next step:\nLikely objection and an honest response:\nNo invented testimonials, guaranteed outcomes, or unsupported claims.\n\n7. ONBOARD, DELIVER, AND RETAIN\nMinimum client inputs and permissions:\nHuman approvals before promises, sending, or payments:\nDraft -> fact check -> quality review -> delivery -> feedback:\nHow errors, revisions, complaints, and refunds are handled:\nRepeat-use measure and follow-up question:\n\n8. DECIDE\nSmallest next test:\nSuccess measure, stop condition, owner, and review date:\nProceed, revise, or stop—and what evidence supports that choice?\n"}, {"title": "AI role and handoff plan — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-ai-delegation", "summary": "Design an AI-assisted operating team with bounded tasks, evidence, permissions, and human approval.", "text": "AI ROLE AND HANDOFF PLAN\n\nYou can practice these roles manually in a ready-made AI assistant or with\nsupplied outputs. Agent software, coding, and autonomous execution are optional.\n\nBusiness process and responsible human:\n\nFor each AI-assisted role, fill in:\nROLE NAME:\nONE BOUNDED TASK:\nAPPROVED INPUTS AND SOURCES:\nEXPECTED OUTPUT AND FORMAT:\nWHAT IT MAY DO:\nWHAT IT MAY NOT DO:\nEVIDENCE THE NEXT PERSON OR ROLE NEEDS:\nQUALITY CHECK AND ACCEPTANCE THRESHOLD:\nWHEN IT MUST STOP OR ASK A PERSON:\nHUMAN APPROVER AND NEXT HANDOFF:\n\nExample: a drafting assistant may propose product copy using supplied facts.\nIt may not invent specifications, publish content, promise results, or charge\na customer. A person checks every claim and approves the deliverable.\n\nHANDOFF RECORD\nInput version:\nDraft version:\nClaims checked against sources:\nMissing information and unresolved exceptions:\nHuman corrections:\nApproval decision:\nDelivery record or classroom simulation label:\n\nFAILURE PLAN\nWhat happens when the AI is unavailable or the output fails review?\nCan the process be completed manually?\nWhat permissions can be revoked, and who can stop the process?\n\nMEASURES\nTotal process time, including review and rework:\nAccepted outputs / total outputs reviewed:\nImportant errors and their consequences:\nCost per accepted deliverable:\nDo these measures support more automation, less automation, or redesign?\n"}, {"title": "Business partner problem brief — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-partner-brief", "summary": "Agree on the problem, boundaries, evidence, and a useful deliverable before building.", "text": "BUSINESS PARTNER PROBLEM BRIEF\n\nPartner or fictional case:\nStudent/team and instructor:\nResponsible partner contact (for an actual project):\n\n1. THE PROBLEM\nWho encounters this problem, and what happens today?\nWhat does it cost in time, money, confusion, delay, or quality?\nHow is it handled now?\nWhat evidence is available, and what is only an assumption?\n\n2. THE DESIRED OUTCOME\nWho will use the solution?\nWhat should they be able to do better?\nBaseline measure and definition:\nOne success measure and one quality boundary:\n\n3. SCOPE AND ACCESS\nIn scope:\nOut of scope:\nApproved information the class can use:\nSensitive information that must be excluded or replaced:\nWho may view the work and approve a pilot?\nWhat tools and sharing methods are approved?\nNo new spending, account access, or external action is assumed.\n\n4. PROPOSED DELIVERABLE\nUsable document/content kit, service workflow, or no-code experience:\nHow AI assists and how a person checks the result:\nNon-AI alternative:\nWhat will actually be implemented or delivered?\n\n5. ACCEPTANCE AND HANDOFF\nTasks a user will try:\nEvidence of usefulness and quality to record:\nReview contact and feedback method:\nDelivery format and accessible share location:\nOwner after the course and maintenance expectations:\nStop conditions and unresolved questions:\n\nClassroom fallback: use supplied fictional data and a peer as the user.\nLabel this as a classroom pilot using fictional data. Report actual observed\npeer feedback accurately, without claiming real partner adoption or customer\ndemand. Use the same rubric and quality criteria.\n"}, {"title": "Implementation, pilot, and handoff — project template", "url": "toolkit.html#template-implementation-handoff", "summary": "Deliver a usable no-code solution, check it with a user, and transfer it responsibly.", "text": "IMPLEMENTATION, PILOT, AND HANDOFF\n\nSolution and version:\nProblem owner and intended user:\nPartner-approved or classroom simulation route:\n\n1. WHAT IS LIVE OR USABLE?\nProduct kit, repeatable service, form, slide experience, or no-code workflow:\nLink or file location available to the intended reviewer:\nRequired access, cost, and device assumptions:\nIf sharing publicly is not approved, use a private classroom delivery.\n\n2. RUN A REALISTIC USER TASK\nStarting input:\nTask the user attempts:\nExpected useful output:\nObserved result (or clearly labeled supplied/classroom evidence):\nCompletion, quality, total time, and rework:\nWhat failed or confused the user?\n\n3. QUALITY AND HUMAN CONTROL\nFacts, calculations, citations, and permissions checked:\nWho approves outgoing content, commitments, payments, or publication?\nException route and manual fallback:\nKnown limitations and stop conditions:\n\n4. IMPROVE AND VERIFY\nFeedback received:\nSpecific revision:\nRetest result:\nWhat is still unverified?\n\n5. TRANSFER THE SOLUTION\nFinal files or approved share link:\nOne-page operating instructions:\nInputs needed for the next use:\nReview checklist:\nNamed owner and update process:\nWhat happens when the tool is unavailable or its behavior changes?\nAccess to remove at project completion, if applicable:\n\n6. MAKE THE CASE\nOutcome compared with the baseline or alternative:\nBenefits, complete costs, and uncertainty:\nPitch: adopt, buy, pilot further, revise, or stop?\nDo not imply a sale, contract, deployment, or partner approval that did not occur.\n"}];window.COURSE_LABELS={"modules": {"0": "Module 00: Orientation: Business Partner Capstone and AI Value", "1": "Module 01: AI Literacy and Effective Use", "2": "Module 02: Business Problems and AI Opportunities", "3": "Module 03: Marketing and Sales", "4": "Module 04: Customer Experience and Service", "5": "Module 05: Business Knowledge and Decision Support", "6": "Module 06: Digital Products and AI-Enabled Service Implementation", "7": "Module 07: Prioritizing AI Opportunities", "8": "Module 08: Responsible AI and Vendor Evaluation", "9": "Module 09: Pilot Deployment and Adoption Planning", "10": "Module 10: Business Models and Competitive Strategy", "11": "Module 11: Customer Discovery and Concept Validation", "12": "Module 12: Economics and Business Value", "13": "Module 13: Executive Communication", "14": "Module 14: Pilot Evidence, Revision, and Handoff", "15": "Module 15: Capstone Demonstrations, Pitch, and Handoff", "16": "Module 16: Reflection and Next Steps"}, "checks": {"assessment-1-0": "Business Case Brief 1: Problem and Opportunity — A specific problem statement naming the affected customer or employee and the business consequence.", "assessment-1-1": "Business Case Brief 1: Problem and Opportunity — A simple current-process sketch and comparison of the status quo, a non-AI improvement, and an AI-assisted concept.", "assessment-1-2": "Business Case Brief 1: Problem and Opportunity — One compact quantitative table with units, period, denominators where relevant, and labeled assumptions; interpret a mean/median or benefit estimate, a quality measure, and two uncertainties. A small dot plot or bar chart may clarify variation. Include review/upkeep effort and distinguish capacity from cash savings.", "assessment-1-3": "Business Case Brief 1: Problem and Opportunity — A short evidence and AI-use disclosure.", "assessment-2-0": "Business Case Brief 2: Marketing and Sales — A customer or internal-user segment, offer, channel, communication objective, and desired next action. Show where the action belongs in a simple acquisition or adoption funnel.", "assessment-2-1": "Business Case Brief 2: Marketing and Sales — Two short message alternatives or one annotated before-and-after example. You may manually revise the supplied TrailBox advertisement; no paid tool or actual campaign is required.", "assessment-2-2": "Business Case Brief 2: Marketing and Sales — A claim-level review of factual support, tone, brand fit, and limitations, with unsupported promises removed or visibly identified.", "assessment-2-3": "Business Case Brief 2: Marketing and Sales — A recommendation supported by a compact count/rate/cost table using supplied fictional campaign data or labeled assumptions. Show percentage-point versus relative changes correctly, identify uncertainty, and propose a bounded comparison; include evidence/AI disclosure.", "assessment-3-0": "Business Case Brief 3: Customer Experience and Service — A customer or internal-user journey covering the need, acceptance or approval, delivery, support, and a useful point for AI assistance.", "assessment-3-1": "Business Case Brief 3: Customer Experience and Service — A sample conversation or storyboard covering a routine request and an exception.", "assessment-3-2": "Business Case Brief 3: Customer Experience and Service — A clear human-review/escalation trigger, the responsible role, the customer’s next step, and what information the handoff conveys. Distinguish the proposed process from an action actually performed.", "assessment-3-3": "Business Case Brief 3: Customer Experience and Service — A compact quality table or figure using the supplied routing counts or a similarly labeled scenario. Explain missed exceptions and unnecessary escalations with their correct denominators, then make a service recommendation and disclose evidence/AI use.", "assessment-4-0": "Business Case Brief 4: Knowledge and Decision Support — A named business decision and a small numerical comparison table showing units, prices or rates, and unknown values. Use the supplied supplier case if desired.", "assessment-4-1": "Business Case Brief 4: Knowledge and Decision Support — An evaluation of a supplied or AI-assisted summary, distinguishing supported claims from unsupported conclusions.", "assessment-4-2": "Business Case Brief 4: Knowledge and Decision Support — A recommendation with explicit dollar/percentage comparisons where supported, alternatives, missing information, and a condition that would change the decision. Preserve unknown values rather than replacing them with unsupported estimates.", "assessment-4-3": "Business Case Brief 4: Knowledge and Decision Support — A concise evidence trail and AI-use disclosure.", "assessment-5-0": "Business Case Brief 5: First Usable Product or Service — A concise offer card naming the user, problem, useful result, included scope, and relevant price or internal resource assumption.", "assessment-5-1": "Business Case Brief 5: First Usable Product or Service — A first usable document product, spreadsheet-supported briefing, form/template process, or reviewed service sample. A prototype interface must connect to a manual output or process that completes the agreed task.", "assessment-5-2": "Business Case Brief 5: First Usable Product or Service — An explanation of where AI assistance and human judgment contribute to the experience.", "assessment-5-3": "Business Case Brief 5: First Usable Product or Service — A three-case test-and-revision record for the implemented artifact, using supplied or permitted inputs. Record actual classroom checks, one revision, and readiness for the later controlled user deployment. Keep provided statistical scenarios separate from observed test results.", "assessment-6-0": "AI Opportunity Portfolio and Prioritization — A concise summary of the five opportunities and material revisions after feedback.", "assessment-6-1": "AI Opportunity Portfolio and Prioritization — A weighted comparison table with four or five defined criteria, consistent score direction, and explained numerical judgments. Show the calculation for one option and vary one uncertain score or weight; note whether the ranking changes. A small score chart may support the same discussion.", "assessment-6-2": "AI Opportunity Portfolio and Prioritization — A selected capstone opportunity, reasons for the choice, and a practical first step.", "assessment-6-3": "AI Opportunity Portfolio and Prioritization — Reasons to defer or reject at least two alternatives, plus a reflection and evidence/AI disclosure.", "assessment-7-0": "Pilot Deployment and Readiness Plan — A specific business question, minimum usable version, partner or classroom test-user role, deployment/access location, task, baseline, and decision the pilot informs.", "assessment-7-1": "Pilot Deployment and Readiness Plan — A measurement table with units and denominators, covering business value, quality, complete staff workload, and numerical continue/revise/stop thresholds. Identify what would be recorded and how averages or rates would be interpreted. Leave result cells blank unless observations were actually collected; supplied simulated data must remain labeled.", "assessment-7-2": "Pilot Deployment and Readiness Plan — A comparison of two solution options and a manual alternative, distinguishing vendor-stated features, tested evidence, and unknowns. The supplied fictional profiles are sufficient; no purchase or real system test is required.", "assessment-7-3": "Pilot Deployment and Readiness Plan — A short risk register and operating sequence from intake through preparation, human review, delivery, and follow-up. Name bounded AI-assisted roles, owners, evidence checks, escalation, and fallback; no agent software is required.", "assessment-7-4": "Pilot Deployment and Readiness Plan — A short deployment/test log template and source/AI-use disclosure. Include version/date, delivery method, access check, user task, actual outcome, feedback, revision, and limits; leave future results blank.", "assessment-8-0": "Capstone Business Proposal and Pilot Agreement — A specific customer or internal stakeholder, problem, current alternative, and value proposition.", "assessment-8-1": "Capstone Business Proposal and Pilot Agreement — A one-page business model covering user, buyer or budget owner, offer/scope, route to customers or internal users, price or funding, delivery, support, costs, and key assumptions. This replaces a generic feature list; it is not an extra document.", "assessment-8-2": "Capstone Business Proposal and Pilot Agreement — Five neutral discovery questions and a concise table of three role-play or supplied-case responses. Identify challenged assumptions, implications, and evidence limits. Include one small quantitative table of supplied concept-test counts or business assumptions, showing denominators and why the numbers do not establish real demand.", "assessment-8-3": "Capstone Business Proposal and Pilot Agreement — The refined implemented artifact or service, plus a short agreed pilot scope: user role, location/delivery method, permitted inputs, task, measures, owner, and partner/instructor approval before the controlled test.", "assessment-8-4": "Capstone Business Proposal and Pilot Agreement — A material evidence-based revision, a short sales or approval conversation addressing one objection and next step, and the next validation question. Role-play is sufficient; disclose sources, simulations, and AI assistance.", "assessment-9-0": "Final Capstone Report: Implementation, Evidence, and Value — An executive recommendation, specific business problem, intended user, and comparison with alternatives.", "assessment-9-1": "Final Capstone Report: Implementation, Evidence, and Value — A concise account of discovery evidence, the implemented solution, and the controlled deployment/user test. Include version, delivery/access method, user role, task, actual outcome, feedback, revision, and limits; place the short record in the appendix if needed.", "assessment-9-2": "Final Capstone Report: Implementation, Evidence, and Value — A simple table of one-time and recurring costs, benefits or revenue, and calculations using labeled assumptions, units, and a time period. Include one clear chart comparing the base and less favorable scenarios. Distinguish capacity gains from cash savings and revenue from profit.", "assessment-9-3": "Final Capstone Report: Implementation, Evidence, and Value — A base case and a less favorable scenario, with a numerical break-even or decision threshold explained. Name the quantity calculated—cash flow, contribution, or capacity value—and keep one-time setup separate. If you report an ROI-style percentage, define its numerator, denominator, period, and whether benefits are realized cash or assumed values.", "assessment-9-4": "Final Capstone Report: Implementation, Evidence, and Value — A concise operating and improvement plan: acquisition or adoption, selling or approval, intake, AI-assisted tasks, human review, delivery, support/retention, ownership, capacity, measures, and stop conditions. Keep it within the existing 4–6 pages.", "assessment-9-5": "Final Capstone Report: Implementation, Evidence, and Value — A brief handoff and learning record: final artifact/location, user instructions, review checklist, owner/support limits, what changed and why, and source/AI-use disclosure.", "assessment-10-0": "Capstone Demonstration, Business Pitch, and Handoff — A six- to eight-slide deck connecting customer/problem, usable solution, evidence, acquisition/adoption, selling/approval, delivery, economics, and next decision. Include at least one readable numerical table or figure with units, baseline, assumptions, and uncertainty.", "assessment-10-1": "Capstone Demonstration, Business Pitch, and Handoff — The usable no-code business product or service, the completed controlled-deployment/user-test record, and concise handoff materials. Demonstrate the actual task and explain how AI assistance and human review contribute.", "assessment-10-2": "Capstone Demonstration, Business Pitch, and Handoff — A specific decision request, key uncertainty, and next step.", "assessment-10-3": "Capstone Demonstration, Business Pitch, and Handoff — A presentation and Q&A grounded in your own understanding, with concise evidence and AI-use disclosure in the deck."}};